WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.053
<c.p>[Begin 4544 Begin Session II.]

2
00:00:02.053 --> 00:00:02.463
<v STEPHANIE DRAGOON>Okay.

3
00:00:02.463 --> 00:00:04.516
Today is November twenty-third, 2015.

4
00:00:04.516 --> 00:00:08.621
We are at Ms. Margaret’s home in Lake Charles [Louisiana].

5
00:00:08.621 --> 00:00:16.011
I’m Stephanie Dragoon with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

6
00:00:16.011 --> 00:00:22.168
I’m here today with Ms. Margaret LaTour-Pitre to talk about her memories of Mossville, Louisiana,

7
00:00:22.168 --> 00:00:29.968
as part of a project conducted in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

8
00:00:29.968 --> 00:00:34.484
Thank you so much for inviting us to your home again.

9
00:00:34.484 --> 00:00:37.358
This is our second interview session together.

10
00:00:37.358 --> 00:00:39.000
<v ENOLA MARGARET LATOUR-PITRE>You’re quite welcome.

11
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.125
<v DRAGOON>So we’re going to start with some more questions about growing up in Mossville.

12
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:45.562
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Okay.

13
00:00:45.562 --> 00:00:49.062
<v DRAGOON>Kind of start again where we left off.

14
00:00:49.062 --> 00:00:53.000
How has the community changed within your lifetime?

15
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:01.485
<v LATOUR-PITRE>There is . . . The change that I noticed was small, it contract, it sometimes looked like we have a full community.

16
00:01:01.485 --> 00:01:07.424
There were new people coming in, families getting married and staying in the community,

17
00:01:07.424 --> 00:01:15.909
and then sometimes it looked like . . . I think it was just the natural order with people as they got older,

18
00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:21.848
and they would rotate out and look like, it would start to get smaller.

19
00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:25.667
And you felt like okay, it’s going to cave.

20
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:34.152
It’s just going to be . . . But the thing that was always interesting about it is that it sustained itself somehow,

21
00:01:34.152 --> 00:01:38.818
and even until just . . . especially until just before the buyouts started,

22
00:01:38.818 --> 00:01:46.030
there seemed to be another infusion, where people actually moving back into the community, building new homes.

23
00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:52.394
So when we first moved to Mossville, myself as a child, that I can remember,

24
00:01:52.394 --> 00:01:59.182
we were at the extreme end of what was then considered Mossville proper near Prater Road.

25
00:01:59.182 --> 00:02:03.000
We were all the way at the other end.

26
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.515
Our house, and the Braxtons, one of the uncles, the Guillory, Mr. Perkins,

27
00:02:08.515 --> 00:02:14.455
were pretty much isolated from the rest of the community, a little distance off.

28
00:02:14.455 --> 00:02:17.000
We were . . . There was no television.

29
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.273
There was radio.

30
00:02:18.273 --> 00:02:21.242
There was the music my dad played.

31
00:02:21.242 --> 00:02:25.485
But we self-entertained each other a lot as a family.

32
00:02:25.485 --> 00:02:30.576
Family culture, the culture . . . my family’s culture was very deep into family.

33
00:02:30.576 --> 00:02:31.848
They entertained themselves.

34
00:02:31.848 --> 00:02:33.545
They worked really hard.

35
00:02:33.545 --> 00:02:39.909
They played really hard . . . but to the other . . . So our area kind of stayed small.

36
00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:47.545
It was like well-established, old, the Perkins and the Braxtons had been there for a very long time.

37
00:02:47.545 --> 00:02:52.212
My parents, as I said before, moved in around ‘46, ‘47.

38
00:02:52.212 --> 00:02:59.000
So, in that area, pretty much stayed as it was for a very long time.

39
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:05.600
But the rest of the community, like I said, there were time periods when we had grocery stores.

40
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.367
There were two little grocery stores in the community that I can remember.

41
00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:13.300
Gas stations had come and gone through there.

42
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:21.367
So, like I said, it was really self-sustaining and thriving little community in that periodically what the community needed was being provided.

43
00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.467
Then after the

44
00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:24.300
<c.p>cycle, they would cycle out.

45
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:32.000
But entrepreneurship on a small scale was one of the things I would say that I noticed about the community.

46
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.108
<v DRAGOON>Can you talk a little bit more about the entrepreneurship?

47
00:03:36.108 --> 00:03:42.269
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Well things . . . Like I said, people would open little grocery stores, little snow cone stores.

48
00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:52.128
But the idea was to try to serve the community with some of the little general and basic needs: the gas station, recreation centers.

49
00:03:52.128 --> 00:04:02.808
So like I said, and then when the school was built, there was this huge building near the Rigmaiden Recreation Center was just an old building.

50
00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:07.737
And I’m sure the history will tell you who it belonged to.

51
00:04:07.737 --> 00:04:12.667
But I can remember as a kid it served as a clinic.

52
00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:20.471
We would leave the school and go there to get our inoculations and all that kind of stuff . . . so,

53
00:04:20.471 --> 00:04:23.347
the shots that we needed for school.

54
00:04:23.347 --> 00:04:26.222
<v DRAGOON>Where was . . . I’m sorry, where was that?

55
00:04:26.222 --> 00:04:30.330
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was near that railroad track at the recreation center.

56
00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:37.313
And I’m sure somebody else that you’re going to interview was more familiar with the building itself.

57
00:04:37.313 --> 00:04:47.172
I remember it was kind of like an old preserved historical building that had been there for a while, from what I can remember.

58
00:04:47.172 --> 00:04:50.458
So that was . . . And as I said before,

59
00:04:50.458 --> 00:04:59.906
they were really . . . I think as a community they would really try to work together, and support each other, and support the community.

60
00:04:59.906 --> 00:05:09.354
It was a diehard there, in terms of trying to keep it alive, and I think they were really successful, like I said.

61
00:05:09.354 --> 00:05:16.337
The contraction, as I said, of businesses and houses, and people who had moved in and out.

62
00:05:16.337 --> 00:05:18.801
But there was always something there.

63
00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:21.266
And the Montgomery Club, that was

64
00:05:21.266 --> 00:05:29.071
<c.p>on Prater Road, that was, again, an example of a businessperson who opened up a business that was successful.

65
00:05:29.071 --> 00:05:34.000
Had some of the big name R&amp;B performers would come through.

66
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.835
<v DRAGOON>Was that the Paradise Club?

67
00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:38.037
Or the Montgomery Club is different?

68
00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:39.872
Or was it owned by . . .

69
00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:48.679
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was probably called the Paradise Club, because, you know, we always referred to it by the person, Valery’s Club, or Wes Montgomery’s Club.

70
00:05:48.679 --> 00:05:57.119
But the proper name probably was . . . [laughs] And being a young person, I couldn’t tell you I know exactly what the name was.

71
00:05:57.119 --> 00:06:00.422
But I could remember hearing them talking about it.

72
00:06:00.422 --> 00:06:05.193
It was right on the main drag, so we would see it often.

73
00:06:05.193 --> 00:06:08.495
<v DRAGOON>Did you ever go there when you were older?

74
00:06:08.495 --> 00:06:12.899
<v LATOUR-PITRE>[laughs] By the time I got older, I got married and left.

75
00:06:12.899 --> 00:06:20.606
But my wedding dance . . . Because there was the wedding, the reception and then the dance, that’s how they always structured it.

76
00:06:20.606 --> 00:06:24.275
So at night, after everything, then everybody would go there.

77
00:06:24.275 --> 00:06:27.578
That’s where it was held, at the Paradise Club.

78
00:06:27.578 --> 00:06:29.780
And my father played the music.

79
00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:38.220
It was the only time I’d ever gone there, because I got married at nineteen and left and was gone for forty years.

80
00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:44.459
During that time period, it thrived pretty good for a long time, and then, of course, folded.

81
00:06:44.459 --> 00:06:47.761
But that was the only time I’d gone there.

82
00:06:47.761 --> 00:06:51.431
We didn’t frequent a lot of those kind of places,

83
00:06:51.431 --> 00:07:02.073
the young girls of my family. [laughs] What we did do is go to . . . Sacred Heart here in Lake Charles would have a Teen Town during that time period,

84
00:07:02.073 --> 00:07:03.908
the American Bandstand type dances.

85
00:07:03.908 --> 00:07:05.376
So my mother would

86
00:07:05.376 --> 00:07:07.211
<c.p>drive us into Lake Charles.

87
00:07:07.211 --> 00:07:15.284
So she was selective about if we did go out, what kind of venue that we could go out and socialize in.

88
00:07:15.284 --> 00:07:23.358
And so as we became teenage girls, my sister and I, primarily, because we were a year apart, we ran like twins.

89
00:07:23.358 --> 00:07:29.229
But yeah, we would go to something like that, a lot of family gathering and dancing.

90
00:07:29.229 --> 00:07:34.000
But seldom did we go to a place like the Paradise Club.

91
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.351
<v DRAGOON>You mentioned grocery stores and the snowball stand.

92
00:07:37.351 --> 00:07:42.378
Were those places you went a lot when you were a kid?

93
00:07:42.378 --> 00:07:48.243
<v LATOUR-PITRE>There was a story that you’re going to hear about, Rougeau, Mr. Rougeau’s store.

94
00:07:48.243 --> 00:07:54.946
Because during that time, he was like the primary store, the primary store in the community.

95
00:07:54.946 --> 00:07:59.554
So yeah, we would go to Mr. Rougeau’s store a lot.

96
00:07:59.554 --> 00:08:05.000
We had a distant family . . . That’s why I need my other family members.

97
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.932
We’d call him [Bay?] as a nickname.

98
00:08:07.932 --> 00:08:11.703
He was also Creole and had moved into Mossville.

99
00:08:11.703 --> 00:08:14.216
I’m not quite sure the timeframe.

100
00:08:14.216 --> 00:08:19.662
But he ran a small store on Old Spanish Trail for a while.

101
00:08:19.662 --> 00:08:26.784
And then things like the little snow cone shop was, again, in an area near a relative.

102
00:08:26.784 --> 00:08:35.581
They just opened up a little . . . Those are the ones that come to mind right now that I can think of.

103
00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:44.797
And then, of course, there was always the mechanics, the guys who would fix cars in their yard or in their houses,

104
00:08:44.797 --> 00:08:49.405
and fix cars for the neighborhood people and stuff like that.

105
00:08:49.405 --> 00:08:52.338
<v DRAGOON>Can you tell me about the canteens?

106
00:08:52.338 --> 00:08:55.270
We’ve heard a little about the canteens.

107
00:08:55.270 --> 00:09:01.973
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Again, not a lot. [laughs] We were really isolated down in that end of town. [laughs]

108
00:09:01.973 --> 00:09:02.392
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

109
00:09:02.392 --> 00:09:04.905
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah, I don't remember the canteen.

110
00:09:04.905 --> 00:09:06.162
In Mossville proper?

111
00:09:06.162 --> 00:09:07.000
Yeah.

112
00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.571
<v DRAGOON>I believe so.

113
00:09:09.571 --> 00:09:10.429
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

114
00:09:10.429 --> 00:09:11.286
Yeah.

115
00:09:11.286 --> 00:09:12.143
Yeah.

116
00:09:12.143 --> 00:09:18.143
<v DRAGOON>Did your family go to the movies?

117
00:09:18.143 --> 00:09:20.714
<v LATOUR-PITRE>[Agrees] A lot.

118
00:09:20.714 --> 00:09:25.000
<v DRAGOON>Where did you go?

119
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.573
<v LATOUR-PITRE>That was the other thing we did for entertainment a lot.

120
00:09:28.573 --> 00:09:36.692
When the drive-in opened up, that was the best thing since sliced bread. [laughs] We would go to the drive-in on Highway 90 very often.

121
00:09:36.692 --> 00:09:42.863
Before that, there was a theater in Westlake, on the main street in Westlake, and we would go there.

122
00:09:42.863 --> 00:09:45.137
My dad was really big into Westerns.

123
00:09:45.137 --> 00:09:49.359
My mom was big into Clark Gable. [laughs] She liked the romantic-type movies.

124
00:09:49.359 --> 00:09:52.932
And of course, like I said, he was totally into Westerns.

125
00:09:52.932 --> 00:09:57.803
So that was one of the form of entertainments that we would do a lot.

126
00:09:57.803 --> 00:10:00.402
<c.p>Like I said, when the drive-in opened . . . Yeah.

127
00:10:00.402 --> 00:10:08.521
One of my memories of the drive-in is in the summer afternoons when the clouds, when the thunderstorms would come up, we’d hold our breath.

128
00:10:08.521 --> 00:10:11.769
It was like, okay, are we going to make it?

129
00:10:11.769 --> 00:10:13.393
The fog and the thunderstorms.

130
00:10:13.393 --> 00:10:20.863
I think I’m fascinated even today with the clouds here in the southern area after a storm, or as they start to build.

131
00:10:20.863 --> 00:10:22.487
Because we would watch them.

132
00:10:22.487 --> 00:10:27.359
And then the big shower would come, and you’re watching the cloud and it’s like,

133
00:10:27.359 --> 00:10:32.880
it’s not going to rain, we can go! [laughs] So we would head out for the drive-in.

134
00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:37.427
<v DRAGOON>Do you remember the first time you went to the drive-in on Highway 90?

135
00:10:37.427 --> 00:10:41.000
Maybe what movie, or any particular movies that stand out?

136
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.636
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I don't remember the first time I went.

137
00:10:43.636 --> 00:10:46.930
But one of the movies I remember, like I said,

138
00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:54.837
other than a lot of the westerns . . . but one of the movies that made a lasting impression on me was Gone with the Wind.

139
00:10:54.837 --> 00:11:02.744
When Gone with the Wind came out, my godmother and her family were still living right there on that end of Mossville with us.

140
00:11:02.744 --> 00:11:06.039
They had built a house where my uncle’s house was.

141
00:11:06.039 --> 00:11:11.969
The Guillorys were back in the wooded area back there, off the main drag, but within walking distance.

142
00:11:11.969 --> 00:11:14.605
We’d go to their house all the time.

143
00:11:14.605 --> 00:11:20.535
But my mother and her sister . . . Like I said, that’s why I guess Clark Gable stuck with me.

144
00:11:20.535 --> 00:11:27.124
They were going to see Gone with the Wind, piled the kids in the car, hers and my mom’s kids.

145
00:11:27.124 --> 00:11:29.101
And we’d go to the drive-in.

146
00:11:29.101 --> 00:11:33.713
And the fog rolled in and shut it down. [laughs] Couldn’t see a thing.

147
00:11:33.713 --> 00:11:39.314
I think a night or two later, we all piled back in the car, went back again.

148
00:11:39.314 --> 00:11:45.903
So that was one of the ones that I can, that stayed with me, because of the circumstances around it.

149
00:11:45.903 --> 00:11:53.151
And so, I guess, I don't know, that would have been, what, early ‘50s, I guess, it came out, something like that.

150
00:11:53.151 --> 00:11:53.481
So.

151
00:11:53.481 --> 00:11:54.469
But we kind

152
00:11:54.469 --> 00:12:00.070
<c.p>of . . . All of the movies that were coming out during that era, we kind of would watch.

153
00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:06.000
If they were appropriate, then, yeah, we always tried to catch a lot of the new movies.

154
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.874
<v DRAGOON>Where did you guys go . . . Where did you get your clothes?

155
00:12:09.874 --> 00:12:11.987
Where did you buy your clothes?

156
00:12:11.987 --> 00:12:13.748
Or did you make them?

157
00:12:13.748 --> 00:12:16.565
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My mother made a lot of our clothes.

158
00:12:16.565 --> 00:12:18.326
My sister and I, especially.

159
00:12:18.326 --> 00:12:19.030
Very much.

160
00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:22.199
That’s why I said earlier about us being twins.

161
00:12:22.199 --> 00:12:25.017
She would make our identical clothes as well.

162
00:12:25.017 --> 00:12:29.243
And we laugh about it now, because my sister was a smaller,

163
00:12:29.243 --> 00:12:35.229
petite person than I. I was a taller, just a broader . . . my shoulders and everything was broader.

164
00:12:35.229 --> 00:12:38.399
And so she’d make clothes and they were identical.

165
00:12:38.399 --> 00:12:40.159
She’d make very nice clothes.

166
00:12:40.159 --> 00:12:41.216
She liked sewing.

167
00:12:41.216 --> 00:12:46.498
She was very particular and very meticulous about her sewing, and it was very exact.

168
00:12:46.498 --> 00:12:50.020
She matched her patterns and lines and what have you.

169
00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.894
So she did a really good job in making our clothes.

170
00:12:53.894 --> 00:13:02.698
But we would go out and we would wear the same thing, and people would always ask us, “Oh, are the two of you twins?”

171
00:13:02.698 --> 00:13:07.980
And we’d go like, “We’re not twins. [laughs] You can see that we’re not twins.”

172
00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:12.206
But it was the clothes, because of the way she dressed us.

173
00:13:12.206 --> 00:13:15.023
So she made a lot of the clothes.

174
00:13:15.023 --> 00:13:18.897
We would go shopping in Lake Charles proper: The Lerner Shop,

175
00:13:18.897 --> 00:13:23.827
Sears . . . [Mullis?] was not exactly within the budget, unless it was extremely special occasion.

176
00:13:23.827 --> 00:13:25.940
Then we would maybe go there.

177
00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:30.870
And then there was the shopping center in Sulphur, a store called West Brothers.

178
00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:34.392
I’m sure everybody in Mossville has gone to West Brothers.

179
00:13:34.392 --> 00:13:39.322
And so a lot of the . . . That’s where we primarily did our clothing shopping.

180
00:13:39.322 --> 00:13:44.252
Other than that she would, like I said, she’d make a lot of it.

181
00:13:44.252 --> 00:13:49.887
And taught my sister and I how to sew, of course, as we were growing up.

182
00:13:49.887 --> 00:13:52.000
We started making clothes ourselves.

183
00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.000
<v DRAGOON>Where did you get your hair cut?

184
00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.000
Did your mom cut your hair?

185
00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:02.557
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My mom’s kitchen or the back porch or outside somewhere. [laughs] Yeah, she primarily took care of it.

186
00:14:02.557 --> 00:14:09.478
The difficulty she had with my hair was that I was one of the kids . . . And like I said,

187
00:14:09.478 --> 00:14:13.121
if you look at it now, you’d never believe it,

188
00:14:13.121 --> 00:14:18.949
but there was a couple of us in the family, one or two of my brothers,

189
00:14:18.949 --> 00:14:25.142
but the youngest brother and myself, we had like very, very thick, I mean, just thick hair.

190
00:14:25.142 --> 00:14:29.513
I can remember her doing the thinning out underneath, in the back.

191
00:14:29.513 --> 00:14:33.884
I remember braids, and I remember her trying to do the braids.

192
00:14:33.884 --> 00:14:40.077
Then as we started to get older and becoming young ladies, we would go to the pressing.

193
00:14:40.077 --> 00:14:45.905
There was a lady, I think she was a Braxton, and she would take me there.

194
00:14:45.905 --> 00:14:51.005
She had a little shop in her home on what is now Trousdale Road.

195
00:14:51.005 --> 00:14:53.190
She used to live on that.

196
00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:57.197
It was Hilltop, I think, they called it at that time.

197
00:14:57.197 --> 00:14:59.747
And she would press my hair out.

198
00:14:59.747 --> 00:15:03.390
So then there was like a day and a half,

199
00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:10.675
or a two-day fight about don’t get your hair wet. [laughs] So it was always a struggle with my hair.

200
00:15:10.675 --> 00:15:18.325
And again, inside family joke even to today about my sister and her beautiful, soft, curly hair with the green eyes.

201
00:15:18.325 --> 00:15:21.603
She ended up with the softer grain of hair.

202
00:15:21.603 --> 00:15:25.974
She didn’t have the problems, the thickness, that I was fighting with.

203
00:15:25.974 --> 00:15:28.524
So I tease her all the time.

204
00:15:28.524 --> 00:15:39.817
Like I said, it’s a family joke about the thickness of my hair and how difficult it was for her to maintain it and to . . . I’d have it frizz all over.

205
00:15:39.817 --> 00:15:48.195
And of course we’d come up with all these nicknames . . . and I don't think I’m going to share those. [laughs] For our shortcomings.

206
00:15:48.195 --> 00:15:50.745
We were big on nicknames—just a sidebar.

207
00:15:50.745 --> 00:15:55.845
I don't think anybody in the house ever was called by their proper name.

208
00:15:55.845 --> 00:16:02.037
There was always a sub-name for us, which made my husband crazy when he first met us.

209
00:16:02.037 --> 00:16:07.137
Anything from like Moose Head, Chubby . . . I mean, all kinds of silly names . . . [Pecan?].

210
00:16:07.137 --> 00:16:11.872
But it would make him crazy when he first started communicating with us.

211
00:16:11.872 --> 00:16:14.422
“Doesn’t anybody ever go by their real

212
00:16:14.422 --> 00:16:14.787
<c.p>name?”

213
00:16:14.787 --> 00:16:22.436
Yeah . . . [laughs] when my parents get angry. [laughs] So my sister was an easy care with taking care of the hair.

214
00:16:22.436 --> 00:16:24.986
I was a little bit more difficult.

215
00:16:24.986 --> 00:16:33.000
But again, my mom was very . . . she was very particular about . . . she always made sure that it was taken care of.

216
00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.932
<v DRAGOON>I’m going to backtrack a little bit.

217
00:16:35.932 --> 00:16:40.958
I know you mentioned . . . you talked a little bit about the clinic.

218
00:16:40.958 --> 00:16:46.404
Where else did you go, did your family go for medical care, healthcare?

219
00:16:46.404 --> 00:16:49.755
<v LATOUR-PITRE>There was a Dr. [Lacour?] in Lake Charles.

220
00:16:49.755 --> 00:16:57.294
My best memory serves when we started . . . when I, as a child, was being taken to Dr. [Lacour?].

221
00:16:57.294 --> 00:16:59.389
He became our family doctor.

222
00:16:59.389 --> 00:17:03.996
Prior to that time, there was a lot of family treating.

223
00:17:03.996 --> 00:17:09.442
Not like traiteurs, although some members of my family did go to treaters.

224
00:17:09.442 --> 00:17:14.468
But if you had a flu, or if you had a cold,

225
00:17:14.468 --> 00:17:22.008
or if you had the mumps, they had remedies and things that they would do from way back.

226
00:17:22.008 --> 00:17:27.034
So we didn’t see the doctor, that I can remember, very often.

227
00:17:27.034 --> 00:17:31.642
I don't think I ever saw a dentist as a child.

228
00:17:31.642 --> 00:17:38.762
There was . . . Like I said, there were things that if you ever had a . . . especially with flus,

229
00:17:38.762 --> 00:17:43.789
they had their remedies that they would treat us with, with flus.

230
00:17:43.789 --> 00:17:53.004
One of the things I can remember was a concoction of Vicks and honey and . . . oh, I can’t remember the third thing.

231
00:17:53.004 --> 00:17:58.449
But for colds or something like that, they would give you these things.

232
00:17:58.449 --> 00:18:03.475
I think my mother’s approach to her house was a big help.

233
00:18:03.475 --> 00:18:09.340
I mean, she Cloroxed everything all the time and really fought to minimize it.

234
00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:18.136
When somebody came home with a bug it was like oh, jump on it, scrub down everything and kept everything clean.

235
00:18:18.136 --> 00:18:24.000
So I don't remember as a small child seeing a doctor very often.

236
00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:31.518
The school, again, took care of the required inoculations, so we didn’t have to go to a doctor’s office for that.

237
00:18:31.518 --> 00:18:35.813
The parents took care with the home remedies of getting us through

238
00:18:35.813 --> 00:18:36.887
<c.p>bugs and flus.

239
00:18:36.887 --> 00:18:42.973
By the time I was ten or eleven, as now teenagers, we started getting more serious illnesses.

240
00:18:42.973 --> 00:18:47.984
My brother ended up with an ankle injury from . . . he hurt it in football,

241
00:18:47.984 --> 00:18:54.786
and then he was doing, working at a bowling alley in Sulphur [Louisiana] and the pin hit the ankle.

242
00:18:54.786 --> 00:18:56.576
The ankle got so infected.

243
00:18:56.576 --> 00:19:05.525
By the time everybody noticed how bad it was, they had, they were starting to wonder if they might have to amputate it, the foot.

244
00:19:05.525 --> 00:19:10.895
But they put him in casts and they treated him for like an entire year.

245
00:19:10.895 --> 00:19:16.623
He was off his feet a little over a year and was . . . and it healed okay.

246
00:19:16.623 --> 00:19:19.844
So well then that had certainly involved the doctors.

247
00:19:19.844 --> 00:19:23.424
I don't think he had a hospital stay with that.

248
00:19:23.424 --> 00:19:27.004
They just treated it with [lamp and composts?] and medication.

249
00:19:27.004 --> 00:19:32.374
Around that same time was when I developed a cyst and had to have surgery.

250
00:19:32.374 --> 00:19:36.669
And then a year after that was when I had the meningitis.

251
00:19:36.669 --> 00:19:40.965
So now, that’s when Dr. [LaCour?] comes in. and we’re seeing him.

252
00:19:40.965 --> 00:19:45.977
We went through that period where there as problems in the house and sicknesses.

253
00:19:45.977 --> 00:19:53.136
I think one of them stepped on, it must have been my sister, stepped on a nail that got infected.

254
00:19:53.136 --> 00:19:56.000
But, so we went through that period.

255
00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:02.202
Then of course Mom, with her pregnancies, she always delivered her babies in the hospital.

256
00:20:02.202 --> 00:20:10.885
The three of us were born in Lafayette [Louisiana], and the boys, the two boys, were born here at Saint Patrick.

257
00:20:10.885 --> 00:20:18.740
So she was certainly . . . like with the last two, I know she was under doctor’s care during those times.

258
00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:23.702
She had complications, not serious, not like life-threatening complications during her pregnancies.

259
00:20:23.702 --> 00:20:26.183
But her pregnancies weren’t that easy.

260
00:20:26.183 --> 00:20:36.933
And then after the last one, that’s when she had the really bad episode where she got, I guess, an infection or something and nearly died.

261
00:20:36.933 --> 00:20:43.135
But that’s when they ended up doing the hysterectomy, once they stabilized her and stuff.

262
00:20:43.135 --> 00:20:50.577
And for a while, she was the one who . . . she had problems, like with . . . I guess female problems.

263
00:20:50.577 --> 00:20:57.606
She did have a heart condition that developed as she got, got worse as she got older,

264
00:20:57.606 --> 00:21:02.567
and which ultimately was the result of her stroke and her death.

265
00:21:02.567 --> 00:21:06.702
<c.p>But there was a period when she was not well.

266
00:21:06.702 --> 00:21:07.529
She’d function.

267
00:21:07.529 --> 00:21:10.010
She wouldn’t stop for a minute.

268
00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:10.837
She’d function.

269
00:21:10.837 --> 00:21:19.933
But then once she did the hysterectomy and they got her on medication with the heart and they kind of stabilized her,

270
00:21:19.933 --> 00:21:23.240
then she just dealt with the heart problem.

271
00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:24.894
But she never stopped.

272
00:21:24.894 --> 00:21:27.788
She was just pushing, going, going, go.

273
00:21:27.788 --> 00:21:29.442
Both of them, actually.

274
00:21:29.442 --> 00:21:33.163
But I guess, in a way, we were blessed.

275
00:21:33.163 --> 00:21:40.606
And we say that often, too, without ever having had like major illnesses or disabilities in the family.

276
00:21:40.606 --> 00:21:47.635
We recognize that was a blessing . . . because I think other than a couple of episodes I cited,

277
00:21:47.635 --> 00:21:53.837
I can’t remember anybody else having a real . . . really serious medical problem, other than Mom.

278
00:21:53.837 --> 00:21:59.625
And then, of course, in their old age, and then when Dad got cancer.

279
00:21:59.625 --> 00:22:05.000
But I mean, when we were growing up, we were basically healthy.

280
00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:07.305
<v DRAGOON>Can you tell me about treaters?

281
00:22:07.305 --> 00:22:09.225
About who were the treaters?

282
00:22:09.225 --> 00:22:11.530
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My knowledge is limited about it.

283
00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:16.523
But my grandfather definitely came from a family of treaters and using treaters.

284
00:22:16.523 --> 00:22:18.060
In Creole, it’s traiteurs.

285
00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:20.748
They’ve talked about it on TV before.

286
00:22:20.748 --> 00:22:25.358
Basically it’s a person who offers herbal remedies for treating . . . and prayers.

287
00:22:25.358 --> 00:22:29.583
As a matter of fact, one time I did have a,

288
00:22:29.583 --> 00:22:39.185
remember had a ringworm on my chin or something and it wouldn’t . . . Mom kept trying to work with it and it just wasn’t going away.

289
00:22:39.185 --> 00:22:43.026
My grandfather suggested that they take me to this lady.

290
00:22:43.026 --> 00:22:45.715
And so we went to Vinton [Louisiana].

291
00:22:45.715 --> 00:22:48.020
I remember being in her house.

292
00:22:48.020 --> 00:22:55.318
And she had me stand on a chair in front of a door . . . Was it a door or mirror?

293
00:22:55.318 --> 00:22:59.159
And she had the salve that she put on it.

294
00:22:59.159 --> 00:23:04.536
And again, I was too young to remember all the details and understand exactly.

295
00:23:04.536 --> 00:23:09.530
But that was the one time that I actually did go to one.

296
00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:15.675
And like I said, my grandfather, we laugh about that, too, both paternal and maternal grandfather,

297
00:23:15.675 --> 00:23:22.589
every now and then they had a little rag or a little salve and they were using it.

298
00:23:22.589 --> 00:23:22.974
They

299
00:23:22.974 --> 00:23:24.894
<c.p>would put it on something.

300
00:23:24.894 --> 00:23:33.344
My father’s, my paternal grandfather who lived next door to us, I think I told you that, that they had moved there,

301
00:23:33.344 --> 00:23:41.026
he had a concoction in a bottle where if you were nauseated or was throwing up or had stomach problems,

302
00:23:41.026 --> 00:23:44.868
there was garlic in it and God knows what else.

303
00:23:44.868 --> 00:23:48.325
It tasted awful. [laughs] But it seemed to work.

304
00:23:48.325 --> 00:23:54.086
If you could keep it down . . . if you could get it down, it would work.

305
00:23:54.086 --> 00:23:56.007
And the castor oil treatments.

306
00:23:56.007 --> 00:24:01.000
They were big on the castor oil treatments and stuff like that.

307
00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:03.233
<v DRAGOON>What are the castor oil treatments?

308
00:24:03.233 --> 00:24:05.466
<v LATOUR-PITRE>A good dose of castor oil.

309
00:24:05.466 --> 00:24:16.258
The worst part is when they would put it in the hot honey tea and you had to sip it just like . . . Much later on when we got older,

310
00:24:16.258 --> 00:24:23.700
it was like it would have been better . . . just give it to me on a spoon and give me something.

311
00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:29.282
But it was in a cup of hot tea and you had to sip it.

312
00:24:29.282 --> 00:24:33.004
It was like it didn’t end. [laughs] It was terrible.

313
00:24:33.004 --> 00:24:41.191
It was kind of interesting, because they did bring a lot of treatments, remedies, that they had learned, and they did work.

314
00:24:41.191 --> 00:24:42.679
They served a purpose.

315
00:24:42.679 --> 00:24:45.656
And then we went completely away from that.

316
00:24:45.656 --> 00:24:51.611
Like none of that works, you have to go see the doctors and get the pills.

317
00:24:51.611 --> 00:24:53.844
And now you go full circle.

318
00:24:53.844 --> 00:24:59.798
You’re finding people are looking for and trying to find more herbal approaches to staying healthy.

319
00:24:59.798 --> 00:25:01.658
I being one of them.

320
00:25:01.658 --> 00:25:03.519
So it’s kind of interesting.

321
00:25:03.519 --> 00:25:09.101
I didn’t know enough about it to qualify it, to say yeah, it’s bona fide.

322
00:25:09.101 --> 00:25:14.311
But it seemed to make a difference for the people who did practice this.

323
00:25:14.311 --> 00:25:22.870
My uncle’s wife, who lived in back of the Guillorys, I keep saying in the back because it was further off the highway,

324
00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:27.336
she was one of the people, too, who would get the leaves.

325
00:25:27.336 --> 00:25:30.685
Sassafras tea and the sassafras leaves were really big.

326
00:25:30.685 --> 00:25:31.802
But she knew.

327
00:25:31.802 --> 00:25:34.034
She knew what berries and what

328
00:25:34.034 --> 00:25:35.151
<c.p>leaves, and roots.

329
00:25:35.151 --> 00:25:37.756
They would primarily boil and make teas.

330
00:25:37.756 --> 00:25:42.966
Or if it was something that was a salve, however they would fix it.

331
00:25:42.966 --> 00:25:47.431
<v DRAGOON>Do you continue to use some of the home remedies that you . . .

332
00:25:47.431 --> 00:25:48.548
<v LATOUR-PITRE>That I what?

333
00:25:48.548 --> 00:25:50.781
<v DRAGOON>That you used as a child?

334
00:25:50.781 --> 00:25:52.641
Do you continue that tradition?

335
00:25:52.641 --> 00:25:59.340
<v LATOUR-PITRE>If I get congested, if I get a cold, if I start finding that I’m getting anything respiratory,

336
00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:03.061
I don’t rub the Vicks on me anymore. [laughs] Horrible.

337
00:26:03.061 --> 00:26:06.038
Actually, I did . . . I had a persisting cough.

338
00:26:06.038 --> 00:26:10.504
It was a bug, it was a flu that was going around,

339
00:26:10.504 --> 00:26:16.458
and a good throat rub with the Vicks and then wrapped it with a towel overnight.

340
00:26:16.458 --> 00:26:20.179
So, yeah, especially, I’m big on the Vicks for sure.

341
00:26:20.179 --> 00:26:22.412
The ginger roots for bad stomachs.

342
00:26:22.412 --> 00:26:22.784
Yeah.

343
00:26:22.784 --> 00:26:27.994
Like I said . . . And I’ve learned in the last few years of her life,

344
00:26:27.994 --> 00:26:36.926
as I would come to visit with her, she would talk and she’d make recommendations, so I’d pick up some of those from her.

345
00:26:36.926 --> 00:26:42.135
But not a lot of them, because I don't know which ones are which.

346
00:26:42.135 --> 00:26:47.345
Sassafras tea was one of the ones that they’d put the castor oil in,

347
00:26:47.345 --> 00:26:52.183
so I don’t do sassafras tea. [laughs] But castor oil I don’t do.

348
00:26:52.183 --> 00:26:53.672
I paid my dues.

349
00:26:53.672 --> 00:27:01.487
I’m not going to do that one again. [laughs] But yeah, some of them I do . . . because they seem to work.

350
00:27:01.487 --> 00:27:06.697
The hot . . . the cayennes and stuff like that, for cleansing, also, I’ll do that.

351
00:27:06.697 --> 00:27:11.534
I’ll tell anybody, I don't know what it’s going to do for you.

352
00:27:11.534 --> 00:27:13.767
It seems to work for me.

353
00:27:13.767 --> 00:27:16.000
So I will do it.

354
00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.667
<v DRAGOON>Did you grow herbs in your yard?

355
00:27:17.667 --> 00:27:19.810
I know you mentioned that you had a garden.

356
00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:21.000
Did you have herbs?

357
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:24.357
<v LATOUR-PITRE>And like I said, my mom . . . No, we didn’t.

358
00:27:24.357 --> 00:27:25.849
Especially not for treating.

359
00:27:25.849 --> 00:27:31.071
And I don't remember her, like . . . Well maybe parsley, onions like that for cooking.

360
00:27:31.071 --> 00:27:36.667
But none of the herbs that she, that they would grow, was for treatment purposes.

361
00:27:36.667 --> 00:27:42.262
A lot of what they used was something that they had learned from their parents.

362
00:27:42.262 --> 00:27:45.992
But it didn’t include them growing special herbs for treatment.

363
00:27:45.992 --> 00:27:53.079
Because Mom was . . . I think Mom was very . . . the word I would think of for her would be progressive.

364
00:27:53.079 --> 00:27:57.183
She had a very curious nature about things outside her world,

365
00:27:57.183 --> 00:28:05.389
and so she was always open to learning something new and to doing different things, and to moving from the old ways.

366
00:28:05.389 --> 00:28:13.968
When they discovered the cyst, it was one of the things Dad kept saying, “Ah, just give her a dose of castor oil.

367
00:28:13.968 --> 00:28:15.460
She’ll be all right.

368
00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:17.325
There’s nothing wrong with her.

369
00:28:17.325 --> 00:28:19.190
She just got bad bowels.”

370
00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:25.532
And she’s going like, “No, she’s going to the doctor.” [laughs] So it became a family joke.

371
00:28:25.532 --> 00:28:36.349
Dad’s cure to a cyst was to give her some castor oil. [laughs] So she didn’t stay in that . . . And I don't know if she ever even really learned,

372
00:28:36.349 --> 00:28:43.063
I don't think, in her household—it wasn’t passed on to her—where they said do this, that and that.

373
00:28:43.063 --> 00:28:48.659
She became aware of it, she knew of it, because of the practice around her.

374
00:28:48.659 --> 00:28:52.762
But it wasn’t something that was really passed on to her.

375
00:28:52.762 --> 00:28:55.000
So she didn’t do it.

376
00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.617
<v DRAGOON>We were talking last time about cooking.

377
00:28:57.617 --> 00:29:03.972
And I was wondering what kind of food and dishes brings back memories today of your childhood.

378
00:29:03.972 --> 00:29:07.710
What kinds of things would your mother make, your father?

379
00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:26.402
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My father finally helping me understand the correct way to cook rice so that I didn't have to be totally afraid every time I would have to cook. [laughs] I think they started trying to introduce us to—“us” meaning primarily the girls—some form of cooking at a pretty early age.

380
00:29:26.402 --> 00:29:32.383
Because my parents were taking responsibility for things at seven and eight and nine years old.

381
00:29:32.383 --> 00:29:37.243
My dad was cooking when he was like nine years old and stuff.

382
00:29:37.243 --> 00:29:40.981
So they would start moving us into those pretty young.

383
00:29:40.981 --> 00:29:43.598
And the frying eggs, stuff like that.

384
00:29:43.598 --> 00:30:00.421
I’m sorry, it brings back the memory of the summer days when my parents were both at work in the middle of the day and it was time for lunch and she would tell us what we could eat—a bologna sandwich with mayonnaise and Kool-Aid.

385
00:30:00.421 --> 00:30:09.393
I don't think I eat those now because of that. [laughs] One of our favorites was also mayonnaise and sugar on bread as kids.

386
00:30:09.393 --> 00:30:15.648
As kids, we’d go in the kitchen, we’d concoct all kind of stuff. [laughs] But anyway,

387
00:30:15.648 --> 00:30:20.181
they did make an effort early on to start showing us how to cook.

388
00:30:20.181 --> 00:30:26.982
One of my nemesis was cooking rice and getting it just perfect, because there’s a way they want the rice cooked.

389
00:30:26.982 --> 00:30:30.221
And most of the time, it was just too sticky.

390
00:30:30.221 --> 00:30:31.840
It was too much water.

391
00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:34.107
And of course he’d pitch a fit.

392
00:30:34.107 --> 00:30:36.698
“You don’t know how to cook the rice!

393
00:30:36.698 --> 00:30:38.964
I done told you to do something.”

394
00:30:38.964 --> 00:30:45.117
Finally one day he’s standing there, and he said, “You don’t know how to . . . ” I said, “I don't know.

395
00:30:45.117 --> 00:30:46.413
I keep trying this.

396
00:30:46.413 --> 00:30:48.032
I never have enough water.”

397
00:30:48.032 --> 00:30:54.509
He filled a pot and he stuck his finger in it and he, “Bring the water to the first marker.”

398
00:30:54.509 --> 00:30:56.776
And it was like a Eureka moment.

399
00:30:56.776 --> 00:30:58.395
It was like, that’s it!

400
00:30:58.395 --> 00:30:59.690
That’s all it took!

401
00:30:59.690 --> 00:31:04.548
Never missed a pot of rice . . . seldom miss a pot of rice ever since that.

402
00:31:04.548 --> 00:31:06.491
And that’s what I’ve passed on.

403
00:31:06.491 --> 00:31:11.673
You want to make a good pot of rice, just touch the top of the rice,

404
00:31:11.673 --> 00:31:17.178
bring the water up to the first inch on your finger, and it’s just about always perfect.

405
00:31:17.178 --> 00:31:20.416
Coubion, the red tomato sauce with the fish in it.

406
00:31:20.416 --> 00:31:22.359
I know how to cook it.

407
00:31:22.359 --> 00:31:28.512
But one of the problem we had there…being the oldest daughter, you was the one who got experimented first,

408
00:31:28.512 --> 00:31:30.779
<c.p>had the first round of these things.

409
00:31:30.779 --> 00:31:32.075
Don’t stir the pot.

410
00:31:32.075 --> 00:31:37.256
When you put the fish in, don’t stir the pot. [laughs] And until I learned that,

411
00:31:37.256 --> 00:31:41.790
it was like, oh, he walked in one day just having a screaming fit.

412
00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.085
“Don’t stir the pot!”

413
00:31:43.085 --> 00:31:46.000
Of course, you’d break up all the fish.

414
00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:49.513
I learned to clean a rabbit with my dad.

415
00:31:49.513 --> 00:31:51.075
We had homegrown rabbits.

416
00:31:51.075 --> 00:32:00.444
And especially after my oldest brother was gone in military, but even before, we learned to clean the rabbits, learned to clean the chitlins.

417
00:32:00.444 --> 00:32:03.957
When they would do the butcher, you’re the gofer.

418
00:32:03.957 --> 00:32:08.251
All the . . . Some of the things, the washing of the meat,

419
00:32:08.251 --> 00:32:16.840
or the hauling it back and forth in the house, cleaning the chitlins, was, for sure you were assigned to do that.

420
00:32:16.840 --> 00:32:18.011
And the gumbo.

421
00:32:18.011 --> 00:32:21.134
The fresh pork was usually stewed or roast.

422
00:32:21.134 --> 00:32:21.914
The rabbits.

423
00:32:21.914 --> 00:32:29.332
The squirrels that he would come home with, and wild rabbits and wild squirrels when he would go hunting.

424
00:32:29.332 --> 00:32:30.893
He grew up hunting.

425
00:32:30.893 --> 00:32:32.455
He was a hunter.

426
00:32:32.455 --> 00:32:39.872
When we were younger, we would hunt more often than as he got older, later on in our life,

427
00:32:39.872 --> 00:32:46.118
because he was working full time at Firestone, and then he started playing music on weekends.

428
00:32:46.118 --> 00:32:48.850
And so he didn’t hunt as much.

429
00:32:48.850 --> 00:32:53.535
But he would bring that home, and Mom usually cooked it up.

430
00:32:53.535 --> 00:32:59.390
Stewed it, roast it, but primarily cook it on the stove, I guess, those things.

431
00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:03.294
So the gumbos, wild game, homegrown meats, stuff like that.

432
00:33:03.294 --> 00:33:05.246
Vegetables were kind of basic.

433
00:33:05.246 --> 00:33:13.444
The big ones were the maque choux corns, the okra, the stewed okra, the way we cook it, not a gumbo.

434
00:33:13.444 --> 00:33:16.957
Okra never went in a chicken and sausage gumbo.

435
00:33:16.957 --> 00:33:22.422
That was a no-no. [laughs] When I first moved away and people said, “Oh,

436
00:33:22.422 --> 00:33:29.059
I love gumbo with the chicken and sausage,” and when I’d hear okra, I was like, “What?!”

437
00:33:29.059 --> 00:33:30.620
But that’s a style.

438
00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:34.134
That’s the way that some people would cook it.

439
00:33:34.134 --> 00:33:36.476
We didn’t do it that way.

440
00:33:36.476 --> 00:33:44.674
It was a couple of times there were chickens and we would get maybe fresh eggs . . . that general kind of stuff.

441
00:33:44.674 --> 00:33:50.920
I’m the one now in the family everybody keep laughing about, “She don’t cook, she microwave.”

442
00:33:50.920 --> 00:33:55.604
But I know how to cook. [laughs] Mom would make cakes sometimes.

443
00:33:55.604 --> 00:33:59.118
But I don't remember like a lot of cakes.

444
00:33:59.118 --> 00:33:59.898
There was

445
00:33:59.898 --> 00:34:09.267
<c.p>a period when she learned to make yeast rolls that that, that was a period when that, we would have a lot of them.

446
00:34:09.267 --> 00:34:12.000
She would make them a lot.

447
00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:13.127
<v DRAGOON>What is that?

448
00:34:13.127 --> 00:34:13.879
<v LATOUR-PITRE>The yeast.

449
00:34:13.879 --> 00:34:15.758
The bread, rolls that rise.

450
00:34:15.758 --> 00:34:21.018
And the thing about when they would make them, the rolls were like this.

451
00:34:21.018 --> 00:34:23.648
They could be even light and fluffy.

452
00:34:23.648 --> 00:34:30.788
But they didn’t make like a small roll like you’d get in the, they were like gigantic roasting pans.

453
00:34:30.788 --> 00:34:32.291
They would rise above.

454
00:34:32.291 --> 00:34:37.176
And figs . . . We had a fig tree, so she would can some figs.

455
00:34:37.176 --> 00:34:46.945
And of course as kids, we were in the woods all the times and we’d get mayhaws, and she’d make that blackberry dumplings, she’d make that.

456
00:34:46.945 --> 00:34:49.952
We’d go pick the blackberries and some pears.

457
00:34:49.952 --> 00:34:56.715
So every now and then she would make like the watermelon rinds, because she grew up with them.

458
00:34:56.715 --> 00:35:02.352
But it wasn’t something she made often enough that it got passed on so that,

459
00:35:02.352 --> 00:35:06.861
like I say, any one of us know how to do it.

460
00:35:06.861 --> 00:35:09.867
We have an idea how to do it.

461
00:35:09.867 --> 00:35:12.121
We’d have to experiment with that.

462
00:35:12.121 --> 00:35:14.000
A lot of rice.

463
00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:20.727
Fridays was always funny in my house, because you either had stewed fish or you had eggs and rice.

464
00:35:20.727 --> 00:35:25.329
Because being Catholics at the time, that was a requirement—no meat on Friday.

465
00:35:25.329 --> 00:35:28.161
So we’d have whatever we ate on Thursday.

466
00:35:28.161 --> 00:35:30.994
Friday there was fish or eggs and rice.

467
00:35:30.994 --> 00:35:37.720
Saturday by ten o’clock my dad would swear he had not eaten since Thursday, and where was the food?

468
00:35:37.720 --> 00:35:38.783
He was starving!

469
00:35:38.783 --> 00:35:41.969
And well, wait a minute, you just had yesterday.

470
00:35:41.969 --> 00:35:43.031
“That’s not food!”

471
00:35:43.031 --> 00:35:45.509
He was a rice and meat guy.

472
00:35:45.509 --> 00:35:51.528
If he didn’t get the rice and the meat, then it was like he had not eaten.

473
00:35:51.528 --> 00:36:00.733
And then one of the things we would also fix on Friday was like, he would fish a lot, too, in addition to hunting the turtles.

474
00:36:00.733 --> 00:36:02.857
Turtle was one of our favorite.

475
00:36:02.857 --> 00:36:04.273
The turtles were good.

476
00:36:04.273 --> 00:36:04.981
And then

477
00:36:04.981 --> 00:36:06.752
<c.p>with the eggs and everything.

478
00:36:06.752 --> 00:36:08.522
And a variety of fish.

479
00:36:08.522 --> 00:36:13.832
He didn’t crab a lot, but every now and then we would get some crab.

480
00:36:13.832 --> 00:36:20.559
One time I remember he and Mom, I guess it was after Hurricane Audrey . . . For some reason there was,

481
00:36:20.559 --> 00:36:23.391
South Cameron, towards Cameron was inundated with crawfish.

482
00:36:23.391 --> 00:36:25.516
They were coming out of everywhere.

483
00:36:25.516 --> 00:36:33.304
People were driving . . . I can remember people were going in the night and basically they were just crossing the roads and everything.

484
00:36:33.304 --> 00:36:39.323
So that was one of the times when I can remember we had a lot of crawfish.

485
00:36:39.323 --> 00:36:46.404
Other than that, we would just go in the front yard and ditch and catch three or four of them.

486
00:36:46.404 --> 00:36:51.006
If we managed to get enough, then we might maybe would boil them.

487
00:36:51.006 --> 00:36:55.255
But we didn’t have like big crawfish boils like they have today.

488
00:36:55.255 --> 00:36:57.025
But we did eat some.

489
00:36:57.025 --> 00:37:02.335
We had enough every now and then that that was part of what we had.

490
00:37:02.335 --> 00:37:08.000
Yeah, that’s kind of some of the main things, main ones, that I can remember.

491
00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:15.512
<v DRAGOON>Did your family ever buy meat from other . . . from a butcher, or did you mostly hunt?

492
00:37:15.512 --> 00:37:15.982
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Yeah.

493
00:37:15.982 --> 00:37:27.720
They would buy it, again, with family connections, they would either maybe drive out to one of their families or, because most of my relatives,

494
00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:36.171
I could say, yeah, maybe most . . . yeah, most of them were farmers, on both sides of the family.

495
00:37:36.171 --> 00:37:47.439
So we would probably get meat from them or Dad might know somebody and maybe buy some of the meat from somebody he knew.

496
00:37:47.439 --> 00:37:51.195
So, yeah, he would buy some of it.

497
00:37:51.195 --> 00:37:59.177
But I don’t remember . . . He liked his meat but it wasn’t like steaks and potato kind of,

498
00:37:59.177 --> 00:38:10.915
it was generally the stews to the stretch kind of pots where . . . backbones . . . There was a period there was a lot of pig feet, and

499
00:38:10.915 --> 00:38:16.549
<c.p>as they got older, everybody started to like wean off of them.

500
00:38:16.549 --> 00:38:22.652
But best thing my mama would make was a pig tail stew. [laughs]

501
00:38:22.652 --> 00:38:25.000
<v DRAGOON>That was your favorite?

502
00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:28.130
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It was just one of those things that was just good.

503
00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:32.682
It was like, I don't know if I would say it was my favorite, but boy,

504
00:38:32.682 --> 00:38:38.657
when she’d come up with a good pig tail stew it was like yeah, it was good eats for a while.

505
00:38:38.657 --> 00:38:41.787
And then one of the things he would do, of course,

506
00:38:41.787 --> 00:38:47.762
was he had his set up with his raw onion was here, and his water was there, and his hot peppers.

507
00:38:47.762 --> 00:38:51.176
So that kind of got passed on to some of us, too.

508
00:38:51.176 --> 00:38:54.021
Not the raw onions, but the hot peppers are good.

509
00:38:54.021 --> 00:38:58.573
One of the things he would do, too, especially during the daytime if he was home,

510
00:38:58.573 --> 00:39:05.117
eat a meal and I guess because he was so busy, he was working a lot, he would just be busy, busy, busy.

511
00:39:05.117 --> 00:39:07.678
And then when he’d finally sit down to eat,

512
00:39:07.678 --> 00:39:13.937
one of his favorite things was he’d drop his head on the table and after a while you could hear him snoring.

513
00:39:13.937 --> 00:39:19.628
You’d take a little quick nap, and he would wake up and gone again. [laughter] But when he finished eating,

514
00:39:19.628 --> 00:39:22.188
he would just drop his head and fall asleep.

515
00:39:22.188 --> 00:39:24.464
And he was a small, wiry little guy.

516
00:39:24.464 --> 00:39:28.732
He was never . . . He gained weight as he started to get a little bit older.

517
00:39:28.732 --> 00:39:31.862
But my dad was always a little small guy, thin guy.

518
00:39:31.862 --> 00:39:33.000
Just hyper energy.

519
00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:35.077
<v DRAGOON>No one dared to wake him up.

520
00:39:35.077 --> 00:39:35.967
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, absolutely not.

521
00:39:35.967 --> 00:39:38.340
No, no, no. Not waking him up, yeah.

522
00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:39.527
That was another thing.

523
00:39:39.527 --> 00:39:43.680
When he was working at, when he started working at Firestone, he was working

524
00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:44.273
<c.p>shift work.

525
00:39:44.273 --> 00:39:50.800
Boy that was an interesting adjustment in the household. [laughs] Because with the shift work, he would try to get some sleep.

526
00:39:50.800 --> 00:39:53.767
And then everybody in the house had to quiet down.

527
00:39:53.767 --> 00:39:55.843
My oldest brother was the worst culprit.

528
00:39:55.843 --> 00:39:58.513
Every now and then he’d start doing something silly,

529
00:39:58.513 --> 00:40:04.743
and everybody started trying not to laugh or make a noise a little bit too loud, and Dad would wake up.

530
00:40:04.743 --> 00:40:09.787
One of his favorite things Dad would do, also, he’d wake up and start cussing and fussing.

531
00:40:09.787 --> 00:40:12.160
“Didn’t I tell you all to be quiet?

532
00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:13.940
I’m trying to get some sleep!”

533
00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:21.357
And then precisely at a certain time of night when he was on midnights, they call it graveyard, you had to go wake him up.

534
00:40:21.357 --> 00:40:25.510
Well sometimes we would go in there and he was awake in the dark,

535
00:40:25.510 --> 00:40:29.663
because the room was dark, it was night, and we had to go in.

536
00:40:29.663 --> 00:40:32.333
And we were told don’t just go in there.

537
00:40:32.333 --> 00:40:35.597
You have to go in and make sure . . . don’t jolt him.

538
00:40:35.597 --> 00:40:37.970
But we’d go in to wake him up.

539
00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:44.793
He’d wait until we got right near his bed, and then he would sit up in the bed or he would say something.

540
00:40:44.793 --> 00:40:47.463
We’d . . . [laughs] He had a great sense of humor.

541
00:40:47.463 --> 00:40:51.320
He was always trying to pull tricks and jokes on people like that.

542
00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:57.253
But we’d come screaming out of that room. [laughter] So you either got in trouble if you woke him up,

543
00:40:57.253 --> 00:41:02.000
or he would lie in wait and scare you if you went in the room.

544
00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:04.192
<v DRAGOON>What did he do at Firestone?

545
00:41:04.192 --> 00:41:06.019
<v LATOUR-PITRE>He started as a carpenter.

546
00:41:06.019 --> 00:41:08.941
No, actually he ended up as a carpenter.

547
00:41:08.941 --> 00:41:16.248
He was just one of the laborers, loading the rubber and stuff like that in one of the processing areas.

548
00:41:16.248 --> 00:41:25.381
I can remember he’d come home and the black gook would be like in his ears and on his clothes, and you could smell it.

549
00:41:25.381 --> 00:41:33.418
And so he was in the processing area, I guess where they were bagging it, processing it and bagging it, I guess.

550
00:41:33.418 --> 00:41:37.071
And he’d come home with this black gook on him.

551
00:41:37.071 --> 00:41:38.533
I can remember that.

552
00:41:38.533 --> 00:41:40.724
<v DRAGOON>I guess . . . What is Firestone, exactly?

553
00:41:40.724 --> 00:41:42.916
<v LATOUR-PITRE>It’s a rubber . . . tire . . . Bridgestone Firestone.

554
00:41:42.916 --> 00:41:47.666
And they manufacture the rubber here, right here at the plant over here.

555
00:41:47.666 --> 00:41:48.031
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

556
00:41:48.031 --> 00:41:50.588
<v LATOUR-PITRE>The polymer, I guess, for the tires.

557
00:41:50.588 --> 00:41:54.972
Because I don't think they make, they don’t make the tires there,

558
00:41:54.972 --> 00:42:01.913
but they make the polymer that they ship it out and then they use it to make the tires.

559
00:42:01.913 --> 00:42:04.836
So he worked there for a long time.

560
00:42:04.836 --> 00:42:07.759
And then he moved into the carpenter shop.

561
00:42:07.759 --> 00:42:13.238
So the last few, several years when he was working there, he was a carpenter.

562
00:42:13.238 --> 00:42:17.622
He was a, they had a day they tore down my house.

563
00:42:17.622 --> 00:42:18.353
Oh, God.

564
00:42:18.353 --> 00:42:20.910
I couldn’t get anybody to buy it.

565
00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:23.467
I thought sure I had a buyer.

566
00:42:23.467 --> 00:42:26.025
I had to finally take it down.

567
00:42:26.025 --> 00:42:31.505
And when I went back, I went there the day they were taking it down,

568
00:42:31.505 --> 00:42:38.080
there were chunks of cement from the porch and the steps where the guy was saying, he said,

569
00:42:38.080 --> 00:42:42.830
“This was . . . ” and everybody who, even the guy who took the house down,

570
00:42:42.830 --> 00:42:48.310
everybody kept saying the house was well built, because he took pride in his building.

571
00:42:48.310 --> 00:42:50.502
He was very meticulous about it.

572
00:42:50.502 --> 00:42:55.616
Mom was meticulous about keeping up her house, and he was about building things.

573
00:42:55.616 --> 00:43:00.000
And so they were like a real complement to each other.

574
00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:08.390
When I looked at all the cement work that he had done and the brick . . . He was a bricklayer and cement finisher,

575
00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:10.678
that was probably his primary skillset.

576
00:43:10.678 --> 00:43:12.585
It was really good work.

577
00:43:12.585 --> 00:43:18.686
Big, thick, he made sure he got a good thick slab that didn’t crack, didn’t break.

578
00:43:18.686 --> 00:43:28.602
The bricks, when they started taking things down, there were some places he still had bricks that they had to oh, just break it all up.

579
00:43:28.602 --> 00:43:30.890
And I went like [audible sighs].

580
00:43:30.890 --> 00:43:33.941
He poured the pads for his pillars, the

581
00:43:33.941 --> 00:43:41.186
<c.p>pillars that the house was set on, because he kept working with the store-bought ones and they wouldn’t work.

582
00:43:41.186 --> 00:43:44.619
So he just went out and made his own.

583
00:43:44.619 --> 00:43:51.864
So like I said, by the time he retired, finally retired from Firestone, he was doing just basic carpentry.

584
00:43:51.864 --> 00:43:54.534
And actually, when he retired from Firestone,

585
00:43:54.534 --> 00:44:02.161
he and my oldest brother and the middle brother started a little refurbishing business on the government grant, I guess,

586
00:44:02.161 --> 00:44:09.788
that my oldest brother had requested, gotten some money for to go out and service low-cost housing and do some,

587
00:44:09.788 --> 00:44:14.364
what do they call it, handyman kind of stuff, the refurbishing stuff.

588
00:44:14.364 --> 00:44:17.034
So they did that for a while.

589
00:44:17.034 --> 00:44:20.085
And Mama, of course, she was a domestic.

590
00:44:20.085 --> 00:44:22.754
She cleaned houses for the most part.

591
00:44:22.754 --> 00:44:30.000
And then, in her later years, she worked for the rectory in Sulphur for quite a few years.

592
00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:30.450
<v DRAGOON>What?

593
00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:31.800
I’m sorry, what?

594
00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:32.700
<v LATOUR-PITRE>The rectory.

595
00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:36.750
The Catholic . . . where the priests live, in their home.

596
00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:38.100
She cleaned house.

597
00:44:38.100 --> 00:44:39.450
She didn’t cook.

598
00:44:39.450 --> 00:44:41.250
The other lady cooked.

599
00:44:41.250 --> 00:44:45.300
But she was the one who pretty much cleaned.

600
00:44:45.300 --> 00:44:52.950
And then her final job was the janitorial job, cleaning offices at the end of the day.

601
00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:57.000
<v DRAGOON>When did they take down your family home?

602
00:44:57.000 --> 00:44:59.481
<v LATOUR-PITRE>You’d think I could remember the exact date.

603
00:44:59.481 --> 00:45:01.032
Just about a month ago.

604
00:45:01.032 --> 00:45:03.823
I finally said, “Okay, go ahead, take it down.”

605
00:45:03.823 --> 00:45:07.235
Because like I said, a lady came through and I thought,

606
00:45:07.235 --> 00:45:16.849
she was all excited and she was going to . . . I was trying to see if I could get somebody to just give me whatever you can for it and move it.

607
00:45:16.849 --> 00:45:18.710
Just move it and refurbish it.

608
00:45:18.710 --> 00:45:21.812
<c.p>And she was all excited that she could do this.

609
00:45:21.812 --> 00:45:26.154
Then she started pushing it out, “I’m not ready yet, I’m not ready yet.”

610
00:45:26.154 --> 00:45:28.325
I said, “Okay, I can hold it.

611
00:45:28.325 --> 00:45:32.667
But I’ve got a commitment with Sasol [South Africa Synthetic Oil Liquid; Chemicals Company].

612
00:45:32.667 --> 00:45:35.458
I’ve got to go to settlement at some point.”

613
00:45:35.458 --> 00:45:38.559
And then she fell off the face of the earth.

614
00:45:38.559 --> 00:45:39.800
She just stopped communicating.

615
00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:45.072
When I would talk with her, she kept talking about all the projects that she had in [?].

616
00:45:45.072 --> 00:45:48.174
I told my husband, I said, she probably got overextended.

617
00:45:48.174 --> 00:45:50.655
She couldn’t come back and close this deal.

618
00:45:50.655 --> 00:45:56.548
Then I offered it to Habitat [for Humanity], see if they could take it and move and refurbish it.

619
00:45:56.548 --> 00:45:58.099
And they looked at it.

620
00:45:58.099 --> 00:46:03.371
Like I say, everybody’s going, it’s a well-built house, it’s a really well-built, it’s a strong house.

621
00:46:03.371 --> 00:46:06.472
They couldn’t find anybody who’d be willing to take it.

622
00:46:06.472 --> 00:46:12.675
Because I think what they do, is if you donate it to them, then they sell it to somebody else.

623
00:46:12.675 --> 00:46:14.536
I think that’s how it works.

624
00:46:14.536 --> 00:46:19.188
But anyway, I got to a point where I just had to pull a trigger.

625
00:46:19.188 --> 00:46:21.049
I had to make a decision.

626
00:46:21.049 --> 00:46:22.600
So we worked it through.

627
00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:25.701
I decided it’s okay, it’s good. [laughs] Take it down.

628
00:46:25.701 --> 00:46:29.113
At one point I thought, I’m not even going to look.

629
00:46:29.113 --> 00:46:32.525
But I actually went the day they was taking it down.

630
00:46:32.525 --> 00:46:37.487
And then my husband and I went back a day or, the next day or so.

631
00:46:37.487 --> 00:46:41.829
I tell them, I say, it’s still a beautiful lot, even without the house.

632
00:46:41.829 --> 00:46:44.000
It’s a pretty piece of property.

633
00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:50.769
But one of the things that my mom would very frequently say and talk to us about . . . And I think about Dad,

634
00:46:50.769 --> 00:46:52.923
and I said, like look, I tried.

635
00:46:52.923 --> 00:46:56.000
Because he was the one who wanted to hold on.

636
00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:58.769
I think it’s kind of typical for guys, anyway.

637
00:46:58.769 --> 00:47:01.231
Mom was, like I said, she was progressive.

638
00:47:01.231 --> 00:47:05.231
She was kind of like use what you have when you need it.

639
00:47:05.231 --> 00:47:09.538
When you don’t need it, pass it on, give it away, let it go.

640
00:47:09.538 --> 00:47:12.000
And so I labored a lot with it.

641
00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:13.846
But I kept hearing her advice.

642
00:47:13.846 --> 00:47:16.308
What are you going to do with it?

643
00:47:16.308 --> 00:47:29.538
Because I had promised myself when I bought the property from my siblings that I wasn’t going to let the property just sit there and run down if I couldn’t take care of it or if I’m dead and it’s just sitting there.

644
00:47:29.538 --> 00:47:32.000
That’s one of those things I was saying

645
00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:32.923
<c.p>earlier about Mossville.

646
00:47:32.923 --> 00:47:36.923
People would start dying or leaving and the properties would just sit there.

647
00:47:36.923 --> 00:47:41.231
And to me, I always thought that’s unfortunate that happens, because they work hard.

648
00:47:41.231 --> 00:47:43.385
They put in a lot of effort.

649
00:47:43.385 --> 00:47:46.154
If you can do something with it, do it.

650
00:47:46.154 --> 00:47:52.308
My big fantasy when I bought that property was I’m going to buy my parents’ property, my grandfather’s adjacent property,

651
00:47:52.308 --> 00:48:00.000
and develop it into a really small little community with reasonably low-cost houses, just if I’d gotten with a developer and made all the requirements.

652
00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:05.846
Well, what I start to realize after I had it was, that wasn’t going to happen financially for us.

653
00:48:05.846 --> 00:48:14.769
We just couldn’t, we weren’t going to be able to pay what we needed to get it outfitted and set up for development to meet all the county requirements.

654
00:48:14.769 --> 00:48:20.615
They came back through with septic system requirements that we just, I just realized that wasn’t going to happen.

655
00:48:20.615 --> 00:48:26.154
So even when Sasol first came with the offer, my first inclination was no, I’m not selling it.

656
00:48:26.154 --> 00:48:31.692
But as I started thinking about it, I said, you can’t develop it the way you wanted to.

657
00:48:31.692 --> 00:48:34.462
You’re going to die with it on your hands.

658
00:48:34.462 --> 00:48:37.846
It’s going to do what you don’t want it to do.

659
00:48:37.846 --> 00:48:40.000
It’s going to just sit there.

660
00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:42.289
So I decided to take their offer.

661
00:48:42.289 --> 00:48:50.137
But one of the things I did do was include a letter asking them, “I can see you’re selling property as you’re buying it.

662
00:48:50.137 --> 00:48:51.773
The signs are coming up.

663
00:48:51.773 --> 00:48:54.716
A lot of it is turning into commercial property.”

664
00:48:54.716 --> 00:49:04.526
I said, “I would just like to ask you to seriously consider if you resell this property and my grandfather’s property that you allow to sell it to a developer,

665
00:49:04.526 --> 00:49:05.507
a residential developer.”

666
00:49:05.507 --> 00:49:08.777
Because where it’s located is still in a residential area.

667
00:49:08.777 --> 00:49:12.701
It’s in an area where most of the people have not sold.

668
00:49:12.701 --> 00:49:19.242
It’s right against a new development called Barcelona, where they have a whole track of houses right next to mine.

669
00:49:19.242 --> 00:49:22.185
So I’m hoping that ideally if they would resell,

670
00:49:22.185 --> 00:49:28.071
a developer could come in and put another few little houses and it would stay a residential area.

671
00:49:28.071 --> 00:49:30.687
And I tell them, I said, “You know,

672
00:49:30.687 --> 00:49:38.863
the fantasy or the hope was to honor my parents’ hard work and what they had accomplished, when I bought it, was to be able

673
00:49:38.863 --> 00:49:41.479
<c.p>to turn their property into something like that.”

674
00:49:41.479 --> 00:49:44.422
So, like she said, we’ve got to move on.

675
00:49:44.422 --> 00:49:49.000
It’s theirs, now, so hopefully they’re going to do something good with it.

676
00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:54.070
<v DRAGOON>What was it like for you to visit the site when they were tearing it down?

677
00:49:54.070 --> 00:49:55.021
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Surprisingly, not bad.

678
00:49:55.021 --> 00:50:01.358
I had a surprising momentary meltdown when I got ready to actually sign the papers after the house was gone.

679
00:50:01.358 --> 00:50:03.259
I guess that was the closure.

680
00:50:03.259 --> 00:50:08.012
What I was allowed to do, my siblings cooperated with me, which I really appreciated,

681
00:50:08.012 --> 00:50:12.765
because when I thought the lady was going to be able to take the house,

682
00:50:12.765 --> 00:50:16.568
I asked everybody to come back to the house one last time.

683
00:50:16.568 --> 00:50:19.737
And we got together as a family and took pictures.

684
00:50:19.737 --> 00:50:24.173
I wanted the pictures to be about the property, not so much about us.

685
00:50:24.173 --> 00:50:29.877
But there was a picture with the three of us, the older ones, when he first built it.

686
00:50:29.877 --> 00:50:35.580
My parents and the three of us took a picture on the steps in front of this house.

687
00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:43.185
So I had a photographer come out and we took . . . the first three of us took a picture in front of the house again.

688
00:50:43.185 --> 00:50:46.037
I just tried to stage a couple other pictures.

689
00:50:46.037 --> 00:50:49.840
The kids, the grandchildren, some of the great-grandchildren walked through the rooms.

690
00:50:49.840 --> 00:50:54.909
My youngest brother was explaining to his grandchildren, where the rooms were and where they slept.

691
00:50:54.909 --> 00:50:58.078
The kids had never . . . the grandchildren had never been there.

692
00:50:58.078 --> 00:51:03.148
And everybody, as many people as possible came out, and I think they really enjoyed it.

693
00:51:03.148 --> 00:51:06.000
So it was a good closure for us.

694
00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:09.849
When I went back, again, I try to be a faithful Catholic.

695
00:51:09.849 --> 00:51:13.698
My thought was bless the rooms because it’s whoever gets the house.

696
00:51:13.698 --> 00:51:15.302
So I blessed my house.

697
00:51:15.302 --> 00:51:20.755
But then when I realized I was going to have to . . . that it wasn’t going to happen,

698
00:51:20.755 --> 00:51:25.566
nobody was going to take it, I think I was ready to let it go.

699
00:51:25.566 --> 00:51:30.377
Because like I said, when I went there the first day, that had probably been

700
00:51:30.377 --> 00:51:31.981
<c.p>a time when I expected.

701
00:51:31.981 --> 00:51:33.264
But it was good.

702
00:51:33.264 --> 00:51:36.472
Once I made up my mind, yeah, let it go.

703
00:51:36.472 --> 00:51:37.434
I was ready.

704
00:51:37.434 --> 00:51:42.566
And like I said, she was telling me what to do. [laughs] She was standing there.

705
00:51:42.566 --> 00:51:43.528
Let it go.

706
00:51:43.528 --> 00:51:49.943
So I think I had worked through some of the . . . and for me, my emotions get more of a stress.

707
00:51:49.943 --> 00:51:53.472
Like I knew there was a period where it was like,

708
00:51:53.472 --> 00:51:59.566
I could feel the tension of having to make the decision and of executing what needed to be done.

709
00:51:59.566 --> 00:52:03.415
And when it finally hit, I said, “Okay it’s done, it’s done.

710
00:52:03.415 --> 00:52:04.377
Let it go.”

711
00:52:04.377 --> 00:52:05.340
Then it’s gone.

712
00:52:05.340 --> 00:52:07.585
I don’t go back over there anymore.

713
00:52:07.585 --> 00:52:14.000
I have no reason to. [laughs] But I’ll check every now and then, see what they do to it.

714
00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:18.664
<v DRAGOON>How . . . Growing up, how did you notice the development of the refineries?

715
00:52:18.664 --> 00:52:21.384
The progress of Conoco, Condea Vista, Sasol?

716
00:52:21.384 --> 00:52:27.603
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Most of the refineries were pretty much in place by the time I born and moved,

717
00:52:27.603 --> 00:52:32.655
we were living, when we moved there and I became aware of it,

718
00:52:32.655 --> 00:52:38.873
most of those refineries were already operational on a smaller scale, maybe, but they were there,

719
00:52:38.873 --> 00:52:44.314
because a lot of them were developed after, during and after World War II.

720
00:52:44.314 --> 00:52:49.755
They brought people in from what we call “The North” to run those facilities.

721
00:52:49.755 --> 00:52:55.197
My mother worked for one of the families who had migrated here to work.

722
00:52:55.197 --> 00:53:01.026
Other than that, it’s provided jobs for a lot of the uncles that I knew.

723
00:53:01.026 --> 00:53:10.742
I would hear them talking about the Cities Service, Conoco, and of course you get the merging and the names change and what have you,

724
00:53:10.742 --> 00:53:20.459
but they kind of go, so I can remember hearing a lot of the men talking about the job opportunities that they had from there.

725
00:53:20.459 --> 00:53:29.009
But in spite of moving here when the plants were already operational, when my dad first moved here, and I think, too,

726
00:53:29.009 --> 00:53:37.559
it’s because of his language barrier when he first moved in the area, he still had a very, very heavy French accent.

727
00:53:37.559 --> 00:53:41.445
And my mom spoke a better English than he did.

728
00:53:41.445 --> 00:53:46.498
<c.p>So the jobs he went after were like still service kind of jobs.

729
00:53:46.498 --> 00:53:48.830
He worked at the lumber mill.

730
00:53:48.830 --> 00:53:54.659
Then he started doing carpentry type work, gofer . . . what they call gofer work at first.

731
00:53:54.659 --> 00:53:57.769
Then starting to build, and stuff like that.

732
00:53:57.769 --> 00:54:02.044
So he did that kind of work for a long time.

733
00:54:02.044 --> 00:54:08.651
That’s where he learned his cement finishing skills and his bricklaying skill before he got to Firestone.

734
00:54:08.651 --> 00:54:17.590
So he didn’t go into the plants until like the ‘50s, where I have uncles who had been there for quite a while.

735
00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:24.974
And then the other issue of that, the people of Mossville, my family, my culture, was the race issue.

736
00:54:24.974 --> 00:54:31.192
Men of color didn’t just rush in, they weren't exactly just pulling you into the refineries.

737
00:54:31.192 --> 00:54:38.576
So it was quite a while before you started seeing a real influx of blacks working in the refinery.

738
00:54:38.576 --> 00:54:40.908
But it was before the ‘60s.

739
00:54:40.908 --> 00:54:42.463
But it was slow.

740
00:54:42.463 --> 00:54:46.738
It wasn’t something they were invited to come into very easily.

741
00:54:46.738 --> 00:54:54.900
I can remember one of my father’s uncles, he would talk about trying to get my dad in where he was.

742
00:54:54.900 --> 00:55:03.838
My dad finally got into Firestone because my mom was cleaning house for the family down the street that I told you about.

743
00:55:03.838 --> 00:55:12.000
And the man put in the word for him, so he was able to get in, to get in there.

744
00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:15.824
<v DRAGOON>Do you . . . What was his experience like working for Firestone?

745
00:55:15.824 --> 00:55:22.708
<v LATOUR-PITRE>His first few years, and I can’t say exactly whether it was his first five, ten or whatever,

746
00:55:22.708 --> 00:55:30.739
but I can remember there was a period where I tell the kids and I tell people I talk to now,

747
00:55:30.739 --> 00:55:34.181
while I do understand the problem that unions have,

748
00:55:34.181 --> 00:55:38.006
or has had, their reputation about being unfair and demanding,

749
00:55:38.006 --> 00:55:47.567
when I saw how my father was working before a union came to Firestone at that local plant compared to what happened after the union,

750
00:55:47.567 --> 00:55:49.861
after they unionized, I am pro-union.

751
00:55:49.861 --> 00:55:55.980
Because they would work, my dad would go to work, and if he had, eight-hour shift.

752
00:55:55.980 --> 00:56:00.187
If his relief didn’t show up, he had a sixteen-hour shift.

753
00:56:00.187 --> 00:56:02.482
If he started to burn out,

754
00:56:02.482 --> 00:56:03.246
<c.p>too bad.

755
00:56:03.246 --> 00:56:05.924
I need supper, I need some food.

756
00:56:05.924 --> 00:56:07.836
It was like, just work.

757
00:56:07.836 --> 00:56:10.130
I mean, you’ve heard the story.

758
00:56:10.130 --> 00:56:13.955
Driving them sixteen hours, eighteen hours, if he’d needed to.

759
00:56:13.955 --> 00:56:19.309
He could have probably worked a double, and then maybe his relief was late.

760
00:56:19.309 --> 00:56:24.280
So that means he’d have to work a few more extra hours . . . exhausted.

761
00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:26.958
And, of course, treated with a disrespect.

762
00:56:26.958 --> 00:56:30.399
I mean, like you’re here to do a job.

763
00:56:30.399 --> 00:56:31.547
Don’t say nothing.

764
00:56:31.547 --> 00:56:32.312
Just work.

765
00:56:32.312 --> 00:56:38.431
When the unions came, the union came in and set up there, they got time off.

766
00:56:38.431 --> 00:56:46.462
I mean, and basically they were abusing the resources to the point where these guys were so exhausted they were accident-prone.

767
00:56:46.462 --> 00:56:49.521
My father did end up with an accident.

768
00:56:49.521 --> 00:56:52.581
He had cut his arm while working there.

769
00:56:52.581 --> 00:56:53.728
Working long shifts.

770
00:56:53.728 --> 00:56:57.552
And so with the union, they got more decent pay.

771
00:56:57.552 --> 00:56:59.082
They got better benefits.

772
00:56:59.082 --> 00:57:02.524
And then they got a more reasonable work schedule.

773
00:57:02.524 --> 00:57:05.966
I can remember that was one of the things.

774
00:57:05.966 --> 00:57:11.320
The confidence he had that if he tried to make a request for something,

775
00:57:11.320 --> 00:57:17.439
or wanted to be treated with respect, was that he could go to the union leader.

776
00:57:17.439 --> 00:57:18.969
So that was good.

777
00:57:18.969 --> 00:57:22.411
Without the threat of having to lose a job.

778
00:57:22.411 --> 00:57:27.000
He couldn’t say anything before that, because you’d lose your job.

779
00:57:27.000 --> 00:57:30.225
<v DRAGOON>What year did they unionize, do you think?

780
00:57:30.225 --> 00:57:31.031
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Let’s see.

781
00:57:31.031 --> 00:57:34.257
I think he started, I guess, in ‘56.

782
00:57:34.257 --> 00:57:36.272
So I don't remember exactly.

783
00:57:36.272 --> 00:57:41.110
But I guess it would have probably been around ‘58, ‘59, maybe,

784
00:57:41.110 --> 00:57:47.560
that we really started noticing a more reasonable work pattern and more respect on the job.

785
00:57:47.560 --> 00:57:48.770
More opportunities, even.

786
00:57:48.770 --> 00:57:57.236
Because again, he had done a lot of work as a carpenter and had some of the cement skills and stuff.

787
00:57:57.236 --> 00:58:02.476
But they wouldn’t, he wouldn’t get those, because those are better paying jobs.

788
00:58:02.476 --> 00:58:07.314
So, they kept him in the loading or the other processing area.

789
00:58:07.314 --> 00:58:13.764
<v DRAGOON>I know from other people we’ve talked to, I’ve heard stories about explosions at the refineries.

790
00:58:13.764 --> 00:58:14.571
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Oh, yes.

791
00:58:14.571 --> 00:58:18.602
I started to say . . . Yeah, I’m glad you reminded me.

792
00:58:18.602 --> 00:58:25.455
I don't know if I mentioned this the last time, but that was like a constant fear.

793
00:58:25.455 --> 00:58:29.084
It’s like when we talked about the integrated school.

794
00:58:29.084 --> 00:58:34.325
When you get to school and you’re already in a state of fear.

795
00:58:34.325 --> 00:58:39.969
So yeah, and even now, for some of the older people in this area,

796
00:58:39.969 --> 00:58:46.419
I think, we still wonder, okay, are we ever going to hear that big boom again?

797
00:58:46.419 --> 00:58:56.094
Because you would hear some of, depending on what type of explosion it was, it could have been really, really bad for the community.

798
00:58:56.094 --> 00:59:05.366
So every time you heard a sound, you didn’t know whether this was going to be the really bad one for the community.

799
00:59:05.366 --> 00:59:07.382
But the house would rattle.

800
00:59:07.382 --> 00:59:11.817
I don’t ever remember like really breaking windows at the house.

801
00:59:11.817 --> 00:59:12.623
Maybe cracking.

802
00:59:12.623 --> 00:59:19.073
But yeah, the house would rattle and then that god-awful sound, whistle, would start going off.

803
00:59:19.073 --> 00:59:20.686
Everybody held their breath.

804
00:59:20.686 --> 00:59:27.136
Because those of us who had families in the plants, until you could hear from them,

805
00:59:27.136 --> 00:59:32.780
no cell phones, until they would finally show up at home, you didn’t know.

806
00:59:32.780 --> 00:59:35.602
You didn’t know how bad it was.

807
00:59:35.602 --> 00:59:38.021
You didn’t know who was injured.

808
00:59:38.021 --> 00:59:42.052
I try to tell that to my young men now.

809
00:59:42.052 --> 00:59:45.277
“I don’t want to work at the plant.

810
00:59:45.277 --> 00:59:46.487
It’s unsafe there.”

811
00:59:46.487 --> 00:59:48.503
No, you don’t know unsafe.

812
00:59:48.503 --> 00:59:53.743
Plants are safe now. [laughs] All of the safety requirements they have now,

813
00:59:53.743 --> 01:00:01.000
and the process . . . the way they process their product now, it’s nothing like it used to be.

814
01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:06.552
<v DRAGOON>Did you hear about a lot of injuries that would happen to people you knew?

815
01:00:06.552 --> 01:00:09.514
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I didn’t hear about a lot of them.

816
01:00:09.514 --> 01:00:12.475
But I had heard of some of them.

817
01:00:12.475 --> 01:00:19.508
As a younger child, because like I said, by the time, around ‘56, I was around ten years old.

818
01:00:19.508 --> 01:00:26.541
So between ‘56 and ‘58 when I started to become more aware of it, once the unions got in,

819
01:00:26.541 --> 01:00:33.204
it was at least a little bit better because they started imposing some of the safety requirements, also.

820
01:00:33.204 --> 01:00:39.867
Between that and then the EPA [Environmental Protection Agency] started to kick in in the ‘60s and ‘70s.

821
01:00:39.867 --> 01:00:48.751
And so then once they came in with the air qualities and the water qualities and the OSHA [Occupational Safety and Health Administration] requirements.

822
01:00:48.751 --> 01:00:50.602
So, you hear of injuries.

823
01:00:50.602 --> 01:00:56.525
There were still explosions after that where you would hear somebody was killed in an explosion.

824
01:00:56.525 --> 01:01:02.448
But I would imagine it probably was a lot worse than that when I was young.

825
01:01:02.448 --> 01:01:08.000
At a certain age, I was just too young to pay attention to it.

826
01:01:08.000 --> 01:01:13.891
<v DRAGOON>Before the EPA, were folks in Mossville talking about the air quality or the water quality?

827
01:01:13.891 --> 01:01:17.940
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I think what happened for a long time was an acceptance.

828
01:01:17.940 --> 01:01:22.358
Because even now, you hear a lot about this is your livelihood.

829
01:01:22.358 --> 01:01:28.985
We’re going to come in and we’re going to make changes, we’re going to buy out a community.

830
01:01:28.985 --> 01:01:33.403
But look at the opportunities this is going to afford for you.

831
01:01:33.403 --> 01:01:37.821
Once black men start being able to start getting into these refineries,

832
01:01:37.821 --> 01:01:43.343
any of the environmental concerns that maybe was there and should have been thought about,

833
01:01:43.343 --> 01:01:52.915
it was like, the more focus was on at least I finally got a decent job; and it was sold that you got a decent job.

834
01:01:52.915 --> 01:02:02.488
I think it wasn’t until much later, when you get a new generation coming in and then start on a bigger awareness of some of it,

835
01:02:02.488 --> 01:02:08.010
because even now, not all the elders would probably say oh, we have a problem.

836
01:02:08.010 --> 01:02:11.692
It would be like leave them alone, it’s job security.

837
01:02:11.692 --> 01:02:13.532
But as times changed and

838
01:02:13.532 --> 01:02:22.000
<c.p>generations changed, people started to go, wait a minute, we need to be more concerned about how is this impacting our community.

839
01:02:22.000 --> 01:02:28.935
My personal feeling is that once that started in the Mossville community, it resulted in what we’re dealing with today.

840
01:02:28.935 --> 01:02:34.830
Their solution to oh, you think you have a problem, is let’s just shut the community down,

841
01:02:34.830 --> 01:02:42.458
because we’re not going to keep fighting with you guys and try to appease you guys and try to pay you guys,

842
01:02:42.458 --> 01:02:45.926
we’ll just buy out the community and shut it down.

843
01:02:45.926 --> 01:02:51.820
That’s how a lot of us feel they managed the concern the community had about the quality.

844
01:02:51.820 --> 01:02:55.981
The air quality used to be pretty bad around here . . . pretty atrocious.

845
01:02:55.981 --> 01:03:04.650
When I first got married and left, and I’d come home, and even now some days when I’m going to work, there’s a yellow haze.

846
01:03:04.650 --> 01:03:09.158
But compared to the way it used to be, it has improved tremendously.

847
01:03:09.158 --> 01:03:10.892
That may be an overstatement.

848
01:03:10.892 --> 01:03:12.972
But it has improved a lot.

849
01:03:12.972 --> 01:03:20.601
But the water quality is an area where I still feel . . . And that’s again one of the problems that Mossville was having.

850
01:03:20.601 --> 01:03:27.882
When you live there and you see what kind of water’s coming out of the taps, it’s like, are you serious?

851
01:03:27.882 --> 01:03:34.817
And the concern . . . And then when you hear actual reports of people discussing actual reports where there’s a plume lurking.

852
01:03:34.817 --> 01:03:42.099
I think that’s another, the refineries took advantage of the fact that the community was not well-informed, was not well aware,

853
01:03:42.099 --> 01:03:48.687
was not . . . was more interested in their jobs than protecting the environment at the time, because it hadn’t manifested.

854
01:03:48.687 --> 01:03:51.461
They figured hey, what, they don’t see it.

855
01:03:51.461 --> 01:03:58.743
So I think they took advantage of that and were . . . It was probably just the nature of business at the time.

856
01:03:58.743 --> 01:04:02.904
Like I said, before EPA got really, really stringent, they just produce.

857
01:04:02.904 --> 01:04:05.331
They’re doing it in foreign countries now.

858
01:04:05.331 --> 01:04:14.000
Because as long as there’s no restriction, they just threw out and trashed out whatever they needed to, and it was no big deal.

859
01:04:14.000 --> 01:04:20.478
<v DRAGOON>Do you know what kind of conversations were happening between Mossville and the refineries?

860
01:04:20.478 --> 01:04:24.179
Or like what kind of relationship they had?

861
01:04:24.179 --> 01:04:29.731
For instance, when the explosion happened, was there a conversation that happened?

862
01:04:29.731 --> 01:04:38.522
If windows were broken, did they pay for them? [laughter] Was there any sort of community conversation going on?

863
01:04:38.522 --> 01:04:40.836
Or was it mostly just . . .

864
01:04:40.836 --> 01:04:42.224
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Business as usual.

865
01:04:42.224 --> 01:04:45.000
<v DRAGOON>. . . go to work, come back.

866
01:04:45.000 --> 01:04:52.389
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Until Mossville reached . . . some of the people in Mossville reached that era, that place where they started making demands,

867
01:04:52.389 --> 01:04:58.611
and it started with the . . . oh, the Oxy . . . oh, gosh. [Brain fart?] brain on that one.

868
01:04:58.611 --> 01:05:01.333
Geez, I keep skipping across my brain.

869
01:05:01.333 --> 01:05:07.167
But a few years ago, the material that they found made national news and everything.

870
01:05:07.167 --> 01:05:14.556
Until that happened, like I said, I think for the most part, you probably always have a small element,

871
01:05:14.556 --> 01:05:21.167
a small group of people who are looking up and asking questions and wondering what’s going on.

872
01:05:21.167 --> 01:05:29.333
But until that spreads out and you get enough people interested in what’s going on, there’s no obligation to the community.

873
01:05:29.333 --> 01:05:36.722
I don't remember them . . . When all of the hoopla started, and they started filing charges and what have you,

874
01:05:36.722 --> 01:05:44.500
the refineries, and especially the ones closer to Mossville community, became more interested in opening up a dialog with them.

875
01:05:44.500 --> 01:05:49.944
Conoco Philips was involved in the situation once before, and they wanted to discuss.

876
01:05:49.944 --> 01:06:00.056
I attended a meeting once at the Mossville community regarding what do you want to do about the pollution that’s in the air, in the air.

877
01:06:00.056 --> 01:06:03.944
And what do you . . . Well, what I heard was: we

878
01:06:03.944 --> 01:06:08.611
<c.p>want restitution, we want to be compensated for what happened to us.

879
01:06:08.611 --> 01:06:14.056
But there’s always that caution of, but we don’t want to make them mad.

880
01:06:14.056 --> 01:06:16.000
This is my personal feeling.

881
01:06:16.000 --> 01:06:22.222
It’s like, we want to make them responsible, but we don’t want to make them mad.

882
01:06:22.222 --> 01:06:25.722
Well, when you’re caught in a position like that,

883
01:06:25.722 --> 01:06:35.833
it’s really kind of hard for you to sit down and come up with some really serious valid, and especially if you look at the community.

884
01:06:35.833 --> 01:06:41.278
If you’re just starting a movement and you don’t have the history, the knowledge,

885
01:06:41.278 --> 01:06:49.833
the experience of knowing how to put this stuff together, they want something, but they can’t tell you exactly what it is.

886
01:06:49.833 --> 01:06:56.833
And I think their concerns were valid, or still are valid for those who going to even stay.

887
01:06:56.833 --> 01:07:02.278
But as long as the refinery . . . So I think the refineries took the position:

888
01:07:02.278 --> 01:07:12.389
well until you can give us very specific ideas of what it is that you want from us, we don’t know what to do for you.

889
01:07:12.389 --> 01:07:21.722
Well, I don't think they were like really community-friendly as they could have been, because I think they could have recognized the people’s concern.

890
01:07:21.722 --> 01:07:30.667
And they knew that there was some cases where there was a legitimate concern, enough to say . . . but it was business as usual.

891
01:07:30.667 --> 01:07:31.444
I’m busy.

892
01:07:31.444 --> 01:07:33.000
I hear you.

893
01:07:33.000 --> 01:07:38.333
So like I said, again, when we get to the point where we are now,

894
01:07:38.333 --> 01:07:44.022
where the community is being dismantled, that became the solution: let’s just shut the community down.

895
01:07:44.022 --> 01:07:48.644
This might be the sensitive piece of information . . . discussion, part of the discussion.

896
01:07:48.644 --> 01:07:54.689
But I think the community leaders were not diligent enough in what happened with the Mossville community.

897
01:07:54.689 --> 01:08:03.222
Having been in businesses and stuff like that, you know there’s always a five to ten-year plan of what they’re going to be doing,

898
01:08:03.222 --> 01:08:06.422
and whether there’s expansions, and what their plans are.

899
01:08:06.422 --> 01:08:15.311
If they have a five to ten-year plan to expand in a parish, in a community, somebody in the parish has to know about that.

900
01:08:15.311 --> 01:08:20.644
Somebody has to have some idea that this is what we’re going to be proposing.

901
01:08:20.644 --> 01:08:28.111
I found it interesting that the police jurors who represent the parish, maybe we had one, maybe, that attended a meeting.

902
01:08:28.111 --> 01:08:30.244
But it was mum’s the word.

903
01:08:30.244 --> 01:08:31.667
They didn’t . . . They could

904
01:08:31.667 --> 01:08:33.444
<c.p>have come out in advance.

905
01:08:33.444 --> 01:08:35.578
This is what frustrated the community.

906
01:08:35.578 --> 01:08:42.689
Because when they finally heard how draconian it was going to be, how they were going to handle the problem,

907
01:08:42.689 --> 01:08:48.378
shut down the community, it was a real shocker, I think, for a lot of us.

908
01:08:48.378 --> 01:08:50.156
This is the best way?

909
01:08:50.156 --> 01:08:53.000
This is how it’s going to happen?

910
01:08:53.000 --> 01:08:58.069
So I think a lot of us felt like where are our leaders?

911
01:08:58.069 --> 01:09:07.818
Why weren’t you talking to the refineries and telling them, “You’ve got to make a better effort at working with the community and cleaning it,

912
01:09:07.818 --> 01:09:14.447
and keeping it clean and safe if you want to stay next to them . . . somehow or another.

913
01:09:14.447 --> 01:09:21.855
Make the community feel like we hear you, we have your best interests at heart, let’s work together, community.

914
01:09:21.855 --> 01:09:24.975
We don’t feel that happen in that way.

915
01:09:24.975 --> 01:09:31.214
And I think if it had, you wouldn’t have had so many frustrated and irate people.

916
01:09:31.214 --> 01:09:40.182
Because you give a good explanation and ulterior motive, or ulterior plan to people, most of them will understand, will accept, I believe.

917
01:09:40.182 --> 01:09:41.352
It makes sense.

918
01:09:41.352 --> 01:09:44.082
You’ve got something you can hold onto.

919
01:09:44.082 --> 01:09:46.811
So I think I’m going off point.

920
01:09:46.811 --> 01:09:55.000
But [laughs] as a community, one of the community members of that community, that’s my personal feeling about what happened.

921
01:09:55.000 --> 01:09:57.364
I think the money moved in,

922
01:09:57.364 --> 01:10:07.215
the opportunities move in at a level where I’m not even sure it’s benefiting the people in Mossville and a lot of the people here.

923
01:10:07.215 --> 01:10:17.460
When I hear there’s an expansion, there will be forty thousand jobs, and then three thousand permanent jobs, what does that do for the people here?

924
01:10:17.460 --> 01:10:19.430
Forty thousand jobs are contractors.

925
01:10:19.430 --> 01:10:24.552
That’s people who’s going to come in, they’re going to make big bucks.

926
01:10:24.552 --> 01:10:27.704
I look at my sons and my grandson.

927
01:10:27.704 --> 01:10:30.857
They’re still fighting to get this minimum wage.

928
01:10:30.857 --> 01:10:34.403
And everybody keeps saying there’s money to be made.

929
01:10:34.403 --> 01:10:37.161
Well, there is money to be made.

930
01:10:37.161 --> 01:10:46.224
But if you’re not doing the contract work, or doing the refinery work, you’re fighting to . . . So it’s like where’s the real opportunity?

931
01:10:46.224 --> 01:10:49.376
I work for Advantage Staffing as a contractor.

932
01:10:49.376 --> 01:10:54.104
My manager personally said, “They keep saying there’s a lot of jobs.

933
01:10:54.104 --> 01:10:56.863
<c.p>But nobody don’t want to pay anybody.”

934
01:10:56.863 --> 01:10:58.439
She’s a placement agency.

935
01:10:58.439 --> 01:11:00.409
She’s placing people on jobs.

936
01:11:00.409 --> 01:11:03.955
She says, “But nobody don’t want to pay anybody.”

937
01:11:03.955 --> 01:11:07.896
So, you look at where is the opportunity for us?

938
01:11:07.896 --> 01:11:09.866
Where’s the advantage for us?

939
01:11:09.866 --> 01:11:17.746
But not everybody feel that way. [laughs] That is my opinion . . . and my little circle, those of us who discuss.

940
01:11:17.746 --> 01:11:25.627
I respect and I admire the people who stay on . . . who decide I’m not selling one way or the other,

941
01:11:25.627 --> 01:11:33.507
because even if it stays spotty . . . And that was one of the questions I asked in one of the meetings,

942
01:11:33.507 --> 01:11:39.812
now that you’ve fragmented the community, empty lot here, house there, how do you put together,

943
01:11:39.812 --> 01:11:46.510
for those who are going to be staying, how do you now join a community back together?

944
01:11:46.510 --> 01:11:48.481
It’s going to be smaller.

945
01:11:48.481 --> 01:11:54.785
But if they really want to stay and they want to hold on to their property,

946
01:11:54.785 --> 01:12:02.666
do you have some kind of plan, some kind of idea, on how you can put the community back together?

947
01:12:02.666 --> 01:12:03.848
There’s another phase.

948
01:12:03.848 --> 01:12:07.000
We’re going to talk about that later.

949
01:12:07.000 --> 01:12:07.612
<v DRAGOON>Alright.

950
01:12:07.612 --> 01:12:11.284
I have a few more questions.

951
01:12:11.284 --> 01:12:11.896
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Okay.

952
01:12:11.896 --> 01:12:18.015
Like I said, I do tend to get off tangents.

953
01:12:18.015 --> 01:12:21.075
So pull me back. [laughs]

954
01:12:21.075 --> 01:12:22.910
<v DRAGOON>No, that’s great.

955
01:12:22.910 --> 01:12:30.254
So I’m going to do a little bit of a time check.

956
01:12:30.254 --> 01:12:33.925
We’re at an hour, twelve minutes.

957
01:12:33.925 --> 01:12:38.209
So maybe just a few more questions.

958
01:12:38.209 --> 01:12:38.821
<v LATOUR-PITRE>Okay.

959
01:12:38.821 --> 01:12:48.000
<v DRAGOON>I’m trying to decide. [laughs] What do you think you’ll miss most about Mossville?

960
01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:57.457
<v LATOUR-PITRE>My own personal secret pride of being part of a black community that thrived for as long as it has.

961
01:12:57.457 --> 01:13:02.186
I think they were a proud people in that community.

962
01:13:02.186 --> 01:13:07.860
And even as I attended some of the meetings for this project,

963
01:13:07.860 --> 01:13:15.899
even then I’m hearing stories from people that I didn’t even realize grew up in the community.

964
01:13:15.899 --> 01:13:25.357
And when I’m hearing them talking about their ancestors, and their connection to the community, I hear the same pride.

965
01:13:25.357 --> 01:13:33.868
I think we were a pretty cool little black enclave there in that we had the Creole culture,

966
01:13:33.868 --> 01:13:39.070
we had the historical people who were there from the beginning.

967
01:13:39.070 --> 01:13:49.000
But I think all of them had a sense of ownership, of this is my accomplishment, this is my land.

968
01:13:49.000 --> 01:13:56.439
I remember when I first moved away from home, when we were living in Ohio, and people were curious.

969
01:13:56.439 --> 01:14:02.704
They wanted to know what was it like living in the South, and about the ghettoes.

970
01:14:02.704 --> 01:14:07.403
And it was like, I can’t tell you anything about the ghettoes.

971
01:14:07.403 --> 01:14:14.842
What I hear about the ghetto, what I know about it, is what you know, is what I hear.

972
01:14:14.842 --> 01:14:22.673
I didn’t grow up in a ghetto community, or a ghetto area—and I don’t mean that in a disparaging sense.

973
01:14:22.673 --> 01:14:29.721
But it’s like an entire . . . I hear people make movies and say, “Well, this is how blacks live.”

974
01:14:29.721 --> 01:14:33.637
And it’s like no, I’m not . . . that’s foreign to me.

975
01:14:33.637 --> 01:14:35.986
I didn’t grow up like that.

976
01:14:35.986 --> 01:14:42.642
A lot of the people in Mossville, most of the people, they didn’t grow up like that.

977
01:14:42.642 --> 01:14:44.600
We were a supportive community.

978
01:14:44.600 --> 01:14:47.732
We were a Peyton Place community. [laughs] Self-sustaining.

979
01:14:47.732 --> 01:14:50.865
And when my dad died was an example.

980
01:14:50.865 --> 01:14:52.823
Oh, people brought in food.

981
01:14:52.823 --> 01:14:58.696
They started, it was like what am I going to do with all this stuff?

982
01:14:58.696 --> 01:15:01.045
People were turning into the yard.

983
01:15:01.045 --> 01:15:04.961
My dad’s funeral was . . . his viewing was just, people everywhere.

984
01:15:04.961 --> 01:15:11.225
<c.p>Some of it might have been his personality, because he was a man about the community.

985
01:15:11.225 --> 01:15:12.400
They knew him.

986
01:15:12.400 --> 01:15:15.924
But that real sense of we are a community.

987
01:15:15.924 --> 01:15:21.014
We have to support each other and help take care of each other.

988
01:15:21.014 --> 01:15:26.104
And like I said, when I look at, they build their houses good,

989
01:15:26.104 --> 01:15:32.369
bad or indifferent, rickety or not, they tried to sustain their houses the best they could.

990
01:15:32.369 --> 01:15:34.718
But they didn’t lose their properties.

991
01:15:34.718 --> 01:15:38.634
They would die and the properties would just sit there.

992
01:15:38.634 --> 01:15:46.073
And the lady who sold the property to my parents, Ms. Davis and Ms. Christine next door to us,

993
01:15:46.073 --> 01:15:51.555
her dress, her demeanor, her house, her property, just, yeah . . . it was good examples.

994
01:15:51.555 --> 01:15:55.079
It was a good environment to grow up in.

995
01:15:55.079 --> 01:15:58.211
And then couple that with my Creole culture.

996
01:15:58.211 --> 01:16:01.344
And again, like I said with the school,

997
01:16:01.344 --> 01:16:08.000
it was a . . . You felt safe in the community, and you felt the support, the respect.

998
01:16:08.000 --> 01:16:14.147
<v DRAGOON>How has Mossville affected . . . or I guess, how do you see it in your life today?

999
01:16:14.147 --> 01:16:16.837
How do you see it in you?

1000
01:16:16.837 --> 01:16:20.295
How do you see your experience growing up there?

1001
01:16:20.295 --> 01:16:21.832
<v LATOUR-PITRE>In who I am?

1002
01:16:21.832 --> 01:16:23.753
<v DRAGOON>In who you are, yeah.

1003
01:16:23.753 --> 01:16:27.211
<v LATOUR-PITRE>A lot of what I think I just said.

1004
01:16:27.211 --> 01:16:28.363
We’ve moved on.

1005
01:16:28.363 --> 01:16:34.895
A lot of the people I grew up with in the community or was in school with,

1006
01:16:34.895 --> 01:16:40.658
I don’t even hardly communicate with them hardly at all anymore. [door opens] Hi, Chris.

1007
01:16:40.658 --> 01:16:41.426
My son.

1008
01:16:41.426 --> 01:16:46.421
But as a family, even when we . . . if we see each other out,

1009
01:16:46.421 --> 01:16:51.032
the biggest problem everybody tease me about is I can’t remember anybody.

1010
01:16:51.032 --> 01:16:56.026
When you leave home and you’ve been gone for forty years, it’s embarrassing.

1011
01:16:56.026 --> 01:16:56.411
People

1012
01:16:56.411 --> 01:17:05.247
<c.p>will come to me and they’ll start talking to me and it’s like I know the face, and they’re calling me by name,

1013
01:17:05.247 --> 01:17:12.163
and I’m going like I cannot remember who they are. [laughs] My sister tease me all the time,

1014
01:17:12.163 --> 01:17:21.000
“Don’t go out unless Cecelia’s with you, because she’s the only way you’re going to be able to know who everybody is.”

1015
01:17:21.000 --> 01:17:30.388
But like I said, I think my culture, my family dynamics and the community helped me develop a sense of confidence, of pride.

1016
01:17:30.388 --> 01:17:36.102
And not egotistical pride, but an acknowledge . . . Here’s a comment I tell my nieces,

1017
01:17:36.102 --> 01:17:42.633
nephews and kids: When you’re confronted as a black person and say why are you here,

1018
01:17:42.633 --> 01:17:51.612
or should you be here, or get an attitude with me about who I am because of the color of my skin,

1019
01:17:51.612 --> 01:17:56.918
my response to them is, “Did your ancestors come in through Ellis Island?

1020
01:17:56.918 --> 01:18:01.000
Or were they here in the 1600s, in the 1700s?”

1021
01:18:01.000 --> 01:18:04.265
I come from a people who’ve been here.

1022
01:18:04.265 --> 01:18:06.714
We’ve been here a long time.

1023
01:18:06.714 --> 01:18:08.755
We helped shape this country.

1024
01:18:08.755 --> 01:18:10.388
This is my land.

1025
01:18:10.388 --> 01:18:18.959
I think living in a community like that helped me understand that, and to develop an attitude about I belong here.

1026
01:18:18.959 --> 01:18:21.408
I don’t have to be afraid.

1027
01:18:21.408 --> 01:18:24.673
I don’t have to convince you of anything.

1028
01:18:24.673 --> 01:18:26.306
Just like I said.

1029
01:18:26.306 --> 01:18:28.347
This land is my land.

1030
01:18:28.347 --> 01:18:34.878
My dad worked this land, he cleared that land, he built himself a house on it.

1031
01:18:34.878 --> 01:18:41.000
So it’s a certain amount of . . . and it’s something you learn as you’re growing.

1032
01:18:41.000 --> 01:18:45.463
Initially when you’re at a certain age, you can’t really appreciate it.

1033
01:18:45.463 --> 01:18:50.669
But when you get old enough and you look back and you can see,

1034
01:18:50.669 --> 01:18:55.504
then you start to really understand how it has played into your life.

1035
01:18:55.504 --> 01:19:03.686
The school I told you about, being in that environment, in that all-black school with teachers who really took their job serious.

1036
01:19:03.686 --> 01:19:04.802
The dress, again.

1037
01:19:04.802 --> 01:19:11.496
You could clearly tell who was a teacher and who was a kid the way they presented themselves.

1038
01:19:11.496 --> 01:19:18.934
They were very proud people in terms of having been able to accomplish an education where they could then educate.

1039
01:19:18.934 --> 01:19:24.512
So I think that helped, and a confidence that yes, you can, if you tried.

1040
01:19:24.512 --> 01:19:26.372
Just give it a shot.

1041
01:19:26.372 --> 01:19:32.322
Because I think there, at that time, the walls weren’t up so much within my community.

1042
01:19:32.322 --> 01:19:33.438
I knew if

1043
01:19:33.438 --> 01:19:37.529
<c.p>I left the community, then I had to deal with walls.

1044
01:19:37.529 --> 01:19:39.760
But the walls were not up.

1045
01:19:39.760 --> 01:19:44.967
So that if I was given an opportunity, or suggested, give it a try.

1046
01:19:44.967 --> 01:19:48.314
My mother was a big give it a try.

1047
01:19:48.314 --> 01:19:51.289
I guess both of my parents were, too.

1048
01:19:51.289 --> 01:19:54.636
I guess maybe that’s where it comes from, too.

1049
01:19:54.636 --> 01:19:56.868
Don’t settle for . . . Go for it.

1050
01:19:56.868 --> 01:19:57.983
Just plug it.

1051
01:19:57.983 --> 01:19:59.099
See what happens.

1052
01:19:59.099 --> 01:20:05.050
So yeah, I think it has shaped me, helped shape how I live my life now.

1053
01:20:05.050 --> 01:20:11.000
The respect, the support, and the confidence to make the effort, see what you get.

1054
01:20:11.000 --> 01:20:11.367
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1055
01:20:11.367 --> 01:20:13.933
I think we’re going to wrap up.

1056
01:20:13.933 --> 01:20:16.865
Is there anything else you’d like to add?

1057
01:20:16.865 --> 01:20:19.431
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I thought of something the other day.

1058
01:20:19.431 --> 01:20:23.097
Talking about memories again . . . Now maybe I said this before.

1059
01:20:23.097 --> 01:20:26.762
One of the best memories was before we had television.

1060
01:20:26.762 --> 01:20:31.894
We probably had a little radio, because my mom was in, she loved music.

1061
01:20:31.894 --> 01:20:35.194
And she probably had a little broken radio someplace.

1062
01:20:35.194 --> 01:20:37.026
We didn’t have good reception.

1063
01:20:37.026 --> 01:20:46.557
Aside from Dad playing and us with the spoons and the rubbing board and having dances right there, just the five of us at that time.

1064
01:20:46.557 --> 01:20:55.355
It was just the first three and my parents, sitting on the front porch in the summer in the heat with the smoke [pipe?],

1065
01:20:55.355 --> 01:20:59.021
watching the moon cross the yard like it was daylight.

1066
01:20:59.021 --> 01:21:06.718
And there was this dog we had who would run up and down the highway barking at the least little something.

1067
01:21:06.718 --> 01:21:09.284
Those were some of my best memories.

1068
01:21:09.284 --> 01:21:11.484
And dad would tell us stories.

1069
01:21:11.484 --> 01:21:19.182
My mom would swear some of them didn’t sound quite true. [laughs] It was . . . And we would sit there for eight,

1070
01:21:19.182 --> 01:21:20.648
nine o’clock at night.

1071
01:21:20.648 --> 01:21:23.214
And then all the lights were out.

1072
01:21:23.214 --> 01:21:25.413
Didn’t want to draw the mosquitoes.

1073
01:21:25.413 --> 01:21:28.713
Then go in and settle in for the night.

1074
01:21:28.713 --> 01:21:29.079
Yeah.

1075
01:21:29.079 --> 01:21:36.044
The other gem that I remember about Mossville—and I hope somebody have enough information— is Pete and Virginia Moss.

1076
01:21:36.044 --> 01:21:39.710
I think I might have mentioned them before, too, with

1077
01:21:39.710 --> 01:21:41.543
<c.p>the wagon and the horse.

1078
01:21:41.543 --> 01:21:46.308
They would go . . . Pete and Virginia would just come along in their wagon.

1079
01:21:46.308 --> 01:21:50.707
There was hardly any traffic on that little road at the time.

1080
01:21:50.707 --> 01:21:56.572
You could always see Pete and Virginia doing their little daily runs, doing their business, whatever.

1081
01:21:56.572 --> 01:21:59.504
So that’s one of our little fond memories.

1082
01:21:59.504 --> 01:22:00.604
And the woods.

1083
01:22:00.604 --> 01:22:10.868
I’m a lot more fearful now than I used to be. [laughs] We’d go out in those woods and just disappear and spend hours picking berries and fruits.

1084
01:22:10.868 --> 01:22:16.000
And there was just so much stuff back in the woods back there.

1085
01:22:16.000 --> 01:22:24.216
So I don't think I would say that I’m going to miss Mossville, because I think Mossville’s going to be there in some form and shape.

1086
01:22:24.216 --> 01:22:28.325
But I think as I, when I left home and I came back,

1087
01:22:28.325 --> 01:22:33.381
because even when I got back, I had family members going like, “Oh, Mossville is dead.

1088
01:22:33.381 --> 01:22:34.329
Mossville is nothing.

1089
01:22:34.329 --> 01:22:37.489
They need to come in and plow it all under.”

1090
01:22:37.489 --> 01:22:42.545
But I don’t . . . And I think because they stayed here, they didn’t see what I saw.

1091
01:22:42.545 --> 01:22:46.338
I don't think they saw it the same way I saw it.

1092
01:22:46.338 --> 01:22:49.498
That every time I came back, it was still here.

1093
01:22:49.498 --> 01:22:54.554
Even when it ran down, and then it came back up and it was still here.

1094
01:22:54.554 --> 01:22:59.294
And that was a good feeling to be able to come . . . because you’re going home.

1095
01:22:59.294 --> 01:23:00.242
It’s still there.

1096
01:23:00.242 --> 01:23:00.874
It’s home.

1097
01:23:00.874 --> 01:23:01.823
It’s still home.

1098
01:23:01.823 --> 01:23:04.667
You could go and you could still see home.

1099
01:23:04.667 --> 01:23:07.511
So now I see patches all over the place.

1100
01:23:07.511 --> 01:23:12.567
But if I can see one or two houses I recognize, then I’ll be home. [laughs]

1101
01:23:12.567 --> 01:23:13.831
<v DRAGOON>Thank you so much.

1102
01:23:13.831 --> 01:23:14.463
<v LATOUR-PITRE>You’re welcome.

1103
01:23:14.463 --> 01:23:16.675
I hope I gave you something useful.

1104
01:23:16.675 --> 01:23:17.307
<v DRAGOON>Thank you.

1105
01:23:17.307 --> 01:23:18.887
You really . . . It was wonderful.

1106
01:23:18.887 --> 01:23:19.519
Thank you.

1107
01:23:19.519 --> 01:23:21.416
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I came from an awesome family.

1108
01:23:21.416 --> 01:23:23.312
Well, I guess I still do.

1109
01:23:23.312 --> 01:23:26.472
It’s a little bit nutty sometimes, but we’re cool. [laughter]

1110
01:23:26.472 --> 01:23:26.788
<v DRAGOON>Okay.

1111
01:23:26.788 --> 01:23:29.000
<v LATOUR-PITRE>I thank you ladies for taking . . .

1112
01:23:29.000 --> 01:23:29.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4544.

1113
01:23:29.000 --> 01:23:29.200
End Session II.]
